WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:07.990
- So welcome everyone.

00:00:07.990 --> 00:00:08.942
Thank you for joining us

00:00:08.942 --> 00:00:12.100
at the "Southeast Asia
Council Brown Bag Series"

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:13.924
for today's panel discussion

00:00:13.924 --> 00:00:16.550
on Resistance and Literature

00:00:16.550 --> 00:00:21.300
Struggles for Freedom and
Justice in Burma Myanmar.

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:25.330
Today's panel will include three speakers,

00:00:25.330 --> 00:00:28.790
Thawda Aye Lei, who has
published four novels

00:00:28.790 --> 00:00:31.040
and two short story collections.

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:33.190
She is currently working as a researcher

00:00:33.190 --> 00:00:35.688
on gender and media related studies

00:00:35.688 --> 00:00:39.460
for Burma based international NGOs.

00:00:39.460 --> 00:00:44.460
In 2021 she enrolled in the PhD
program in political science

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:47.800
at McMaster University Canada.

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:49.493
Welcome Thawda Aye Lei.

00:00:50.530 --> 00:00:55.490
Ko Ko Thett is a Burma born
poet, literary translator

00:00:55.490 --> 00:00:58.950
and poetry editor for "Mekong Review."

00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:02.430
He started writing poems
for samizdat pamphlet

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:06.790
at Yangon Institute of
Technology in the 1990s.

00:01:06.790 --> 00:01:11.360
After a brush with the authorities
in 1996, student protest,

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:14.100
he left Burma in 1997

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:17.300
and has led an itinerant life ever since.

00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:19.720
Ko Ko Thett has published and edited

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:21.590
several collections of poetry

00:01:21.590 --> 00:01:24.520
and translations in both
Burmese and English.

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:29.210
His most recent poetry
collection is "Bamboophobia,"

00:01:29.210 --> 00:01:30.540
from Zephyr Press.

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:33.910
He lives in Norwich of the UK.

00:01:33.910 --> 00:01:38.010
Dr. Ma Thida, is currently a
visiting research associate

00:01:38.010 --> 00:01:41.290
at the Council on Southeast
Asian Studies here at Yale,

00:01:41.290 --> 00:01:45.650
and she's also this spearhead
organizer of this panel.

00:01:45.650 --> 00:01:47.960
She's published more than 25 books,

00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:50.010
including a prison memoir.

00:01:50.010 --> 00:01:52.720
She is also an alumni of writing programs

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:55.590
of the University of
Iowa, Brown University,

00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:58.740
and of Radcliffe Fellowship
at Harvard university.

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:01.420
She is recently elected as the chair

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:05.890
of the Writer in Prison
Committee of Penn International.

00:02:05.890 --> 00:02:09.239
Please join me in welcoming
all three of these panelists.

00:02:09.239 --> 00:02:12.420
So what we'll follow from this
point on for a little bit,

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:15.030
we'll open a kind of open discussion

00:02:15.030 --> 00:02:19.260
about the literary life
of writers in Myanmar.

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:22.030
And what I'll do is pose a few questions

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:23.220
that each of the panelists

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:25.709
will kind of go through in a series

00:02:25.709 --> 00:02:28.940
to talk and reflect on
some of their experiences,

00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:32.000
and then we'll share a piece
of writing from each of them,

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:35.100
and then we'll have an
open Q&amp;A after that.

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:37.857
So the first question I wanna ask

00:02:37.857 --> 00:02:39.920
for all three of you

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:42.400
is really just to take us

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:45.370
into the literary world of Myanmar,

00:02:45.370 --> 00:02:48.513
when you discovered yourself as a writer.

00:02:49.430 --> 00:02:51.210
How did you become a writer?

00:02:51.210 --> 00:02:54.170
And what were your influences
when you were a writer?

00:02:54.170 --> 00:02:55.820
As a young reader,

00:02:55.820 --> 00:02:58.470
thinking about the literary world,

00:02:58.470 --> 00:03:00.130
who were you reading,

00:03:00.130 --> 00:03:01.530
who you thinking about,

00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:05.270
and what kind of conditions
were you facing as a writer?

00:03:05.270 --> 00:03:07.483
So I'll start with Ma Thida.

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:10.450
- Thank you, thank you everybody,

00:03:10.450 --> 00:03:11.283
thank you, Eric.

00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:17.980
I think I became a very, very
voracious reader at first.

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:23.560
So reading makes me pretty much inspiring.

00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:25.160
And then after that,

00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:30.160
I think I also want to share
some of my thoughts, opinions,

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:35.360
and my writing skills with the readers,

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:37.140
then I became a writer.

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:38.530
That's what I'm thinking,

00:03:38.530 --> 00:03:43.530
because reading, it's
like a new world to me,

00:03:44.802 --> 00:03:46.930
as a teenager,

00:03:46.930 --> 00:03:50.540
so I have been reading a lot in these days

00:03:50.540 --> 00:03:53.280
because of these very heavy censorship.

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:58.040
The most available foreign
literature is Soviet literature.

00:03:58.040 --> 00:04:02.920
So I have been pretty much like

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:05.700
that kind of the literature

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:10.700
it's already having more
on the war and peace.

00:04:15.850 --> 00:04:19.430
- Ma Thida, you became
muted, unfortunately.

00:04:19.430 --> 00:04:21.020
- Oh, sorry.

00:04:21.020 --> 00:04:23.080
- You said war and peace
and then you got muted.

00:04:23.080 --> 00:04:25.430
- Oh, sorry.

00:04:25.430 --> 00:04:27.590
Yeah, more like in that sense,

00:04:27.590 --> 00:04:31.170
war peace or revolution,
independence, fighting,

00:04:31.170 --> 00:04:32.350
something like that.

00:04:32.350 --> 00:04:37.247
So I pretty much follow
this kind of the writing

00:04:38.140 --> 00:04:42.130
and the literature,

00:04:42.130 --> 00:04:45.440
more on the personal autobiography

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:47.810
of the independence fighters,

00:04:47.810 --> 00:04:49.390
not just from my country,

00:04:49.390 --> 00:04:52.520
but also from all over the
world, something like that.

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:56.727
And then I also pretty much reading

00:04:58.280 --> 00:05:02.920
most of the all writings too,

00:05:02.920 --> 00:05:07.920
because there is all most
only available literature

00:05:09.560 --> 00:05:12.270
I can get access to,

00:05:12.270 --> 00:05:15.940
even with that it's
pretty much hard for us.

00:05:15.940 --> 00:05:20.940
So I interested in also
with my own experience.

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:27.320
Both of my parents were not
from Yangon where we live,

00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:29.850
they were from the other states

00:05:29.850 --> 00:05:31.910
where the other ethnic minorities are.

00:05:31.910 --> 00:05:34.540
And both of my parents are
not the Myanmar majority,

00:05:34.540 --> 00:05:36.314
they are not the Myanmar.

00:05:36.314 --> 00:05:40.340
So I have more experience
in Shan State and Mon State.

00:05:40.340 --> 00:05:45.340
And every summer we visited,
especially to Mon State,

00:05:45.740 --> 00:05:47.480
but sometimes to Shan State.

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:51.760
So I think because of my parents,

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:55.160
our families has been
traveling inside the country,

00:05:55.160 --> 00:05:58.803
and I notice the general public,

00:06:00.710 --> 00:06:03.810
everything is not very the same,

00:06:03.810 --> 00:06:07.380
and the lives in the
other parts of the country

00:06:07.380 --> 00:06:10.530
is very different from where we do live.

00:06:10.530 --> 00:06:12.600
With that notion, I'm interested in

00:06:15.930 --> 00:06:18.910
explaining or telling about their stories,

00:06:18.910 --> 00:06:20.220
but at the same time,

00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:22.730
I fall in love with the...

00:06:24.934 --> 00:06:26.358
What should I say?

00:06:26.358 --> 00:06:27.520
The language itself.

00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:30.540
The grammar structure, the vocabularies,

00:06:30.540 --> 00:06:32.470
manipulation of the vocabularies.

00:06:32.470 --> 00:06:35.440
And the nature of the
Burmese language itself

00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:40.300
has very rich in ambiguous vocabulary.

00:06:40.300 --> 00:06:45.300
So many aspect of the linguists
make me wonder in writing

00:06:47.810 --> 00:06:48.643
that kind of thing.

00:06:48.643 --> 00:06:53.260
So I think for me, it's pretty
much mixed in that sense,

00:06:53.260 --> 00:06:54.800
becoming a writer,

00:06:54.800 --> 00:06:58.380
trying to talk about what's
going on on the ground

00:06:59.700 --> 00:07:02.740
and noticing the resistance

00:07:02.740 --> 00:07:06.890
and independence fighting of the past.

00:07:06.890 --> 00:07:08.920
At the same time,

00:07:08.920 --> 00:07:11.210
I have big notion

00:07:11.210 --> 00:07:16.210
about the newest aspect
of the literature too.

00:07:16.210 --> 00:07:19.540
But with that one,

00:07:19.540 --> 00:07:24.540
I really wrote a lot during
my medical school days,

00:07:25.970 --> 00:07:28.780
but I have experience, I might say,

00:07:28.780 --> 00:07:31.060
to publish one of my short stories,

00:07:31.060 --> 00:07:33.046
it took me 12 years.

00:07:33.046 --> 00:07:37.370
To publish a novel, it took me 10 years,

00:07:37.370 --> 00:07:42.020
and to publish book, it took me 30 years.

00:07:42.020 --> 00:07:47.020
So for me, writing is not
directly related to publishing.

00:07:47.620 --> 00:07:52.620
So without expecting much on
having chance to get published,

00:07:54.610 --> 00:07:55.620
I keep writing.

00:07:55.620 --> 00:07:59.860
That might be my decision throughout.

00:07:59.860 --> 00:08:03.490
I just want to write
and I wrote that's all.

00:08:03.490 --> 00:08:08.490
So the experience as a writer
Myanmar in this day was-

00:08:12.200 --> 00:08:14.152
- You became muted again.

00:08:14.152 --> 00:08:16.300
- I didn't do anything.

00:08:16.300 --> 00:08:20.480
Maybe it's a system make me.

00:08:20.480 --> 00:08:23.143
Sorry about that, yeah.

00:08:25.410 --> 00:08:27.100
- Well, we'll use this as an opportunity

00:08:27.100 --> 00:08:28.523
to turn to Ko Ko Thett.

00:08:29.390 --> 00:08:30.530
I hope that the muting

00:08:30.530 --> 00:08:34.820
is not a function of the
censorship you've experienced

00:08:34.820 --> 00:08:36.686
in Myanmar sometime.

00:08:36.686 --> 00:08:39.900
Ko Ko Thett, please tell
us about your experience

00:08:39.900 --> 00:08:42.693
entering to the world as a writer.

00:08:43.750 --> 00:08:47.190
- Well, I have had somewhat,

00:08:47.190 --> 00:08:50.480
slightly different
experience than Ma Thida,

00:08:50.480 --> 00:08:52.880
because, well, I was a key writer

00:08:54.230 --> 00:08:56.160
during my formative year,

00:08:56.160 --> 00:08:58.720
but I didn't know what
I would like to write,

00:08:58.720 --> 00:09:01.730
but it was only at the campus

00:09:01.730 --> 00:09:03.730
of Yangon Institute of Technology,

00:09:03.730 --> 00:09:08.710
which was and remains
hub for student activism.

00:09:08.710 --> 00:09:13.710
I became a serious poet, a samizdat poet.

00:09:14.600 --> 00:09:19.530
We had a cycle of students

00:09:19.530 --> 00:09:22.780
who liked to publish our own poems,

00:09:22.780 --> 00:09:24.820
not necessarily political poems,

00:09:24.820 --> 00:09:27.040
but we would like to
publish our own poems,

00:09:27.040 --> 00:09:27.873
in our own way,

00:09:27.873 --> 00:09:32.873
without having to negotiate
the repressive censorship,

00:09:33.590 --> 00:09:37.260
that was prevalent in the
early 1990s in Myanmar.

00:09:37.260 --> 00:09:42.120
So Ma Thida says it took years
for her to get published,

00:09:42.120 --> 00:09:43.700
but it was very easy for us,

00:09:43.700 --> 00:09:45.020
because it was self-published.

00:09:45.020 --> 00:09:47.760
And the pamphlets, they
are not even books,

00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:48.760
they are not even checkbooks,

00:09:48.760 --> 00:09:50.620
they are really small pamphlets.

00:09:50.620 --> 00:09:54.620
So that's how I became really into poetry.

00:09:54.620 --> 00:09:59.400
And I think that was my first
experience of publishing.

00:09:59.400 --> 00:10:02.863
Yeah, kind of self-published
poet to begin with.

00:10:03.920 --> 00:10:06.460
- Ko Ko Thett, might I ask you,

00:10:06.460 --> 00:10:07.908
this is very interesting to hear about

00:10:07.908 --> 00:10:10.233
the way in which you started writing.

00:10:11.240 --> 00:10:16.240
What drove you to choose poetry
as your preferred medium?

00:10:16.820 --> 00:10:18.716
What was it that attracted you to poetry?

00:10:18.716 --> 00:10:20.520
Was there something going on

00:10:20.520 --> 00:10:24.910
in the exchange of other
writers in the samizdat world

00:10:24.910 --> 00:10:26.730
or something else that inspired you

00:10:26.730 --> 00:10:29.210
through poetry in particular?

00:10:29.210 --> 00:10:34.000
- Yes, I was exposed to
this samizdat circle.

00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:37.060
Most of them write poems.

00:10:37.060 --> 00:10:37.990
And also, of course,

00:10:37.990 --> 00:10:40.300
if you would like to publish as samizdat,

00:10:40.300 --> 00:10:44.053
I mean, no other literary
form will be practical,

00:10:45.440 --> 00:10:47.470
because the book has to be really slim

00:10:47.470 --> 00:10:52.470
and will be, well, the pamphlet size,

00:10:52.470 --> 00:10:54.525
we have to be slim and we have to be able

00:10:54.525 --> 00:10:56.893
to be hidden into another book.

00:10:57.840 --> 00:11:00.390
So poetry was a practical choice.

00:11:00.390 --> 00:11:03.040
And also of course, I loved poetry

00:11:03.040 --> 00:11:05.990
and I was inspired by
contemporary poets of Myanmar,

00:11:05.990 --> 00:11:08.647
like Khin Zaw Myint, Aung Phyo Way

00:11:08.647 --> 00:11:10.090
and Maung Pyiyt Min,

00:11:10.090 --> 00:11:14.233
who I translated all over
the first 10, 20 years, yeah.

00:11:16.910 --> 00:11:18.900
- Just wanted to ask one
more question on this

00:11:18.900 --> 00:11:20.680
and we'll move on a little,

00:11:20.680 --> 00:11:23.440
but could you describe the conditions

00:11:23.440 --> 00:11:25.770
of samizdat publishing in term...

00:11:25.770 --> 00:11:30.220
You mentioned the size of the
pamphlet needing to be slim.

00:11:30.220 --> 00:11:34.720
I imagine that acquiring paper
and finding means to print,

00:11:34.720 --> 00:11:36.850
were not as straightforward
as they might be

00:11:36.850 --> 00:11:40.420
say in contemporary New
Haven where I am right now.

00:11:40.420 --> 00:11:42.460
What were some of the
challenges that you faced

00:11:42.460 --> 00:11:45.550
to actually produce
some of these pamphlets?

00:11:45.550 --> 00:11:48.670
- Yes, we had had several challenges,

00:11:48.670 --> 00:11:50.610
but my times were a little easier,

00:11:50.610 --> 00:11:54.300
because I have seen some
earlier samizdat publications

00:11:54.300 --> 00:11:58.230
with which were printed over
Gestetner kind of press,

00:11:58.230 --> 00:11:59.770
which is through hand ruled,

00:11:59.770 --> 00:12:04.600
and sometimes typed and hand ruled

00:12:04.600 --> 00:12:06.010
and hand copied and distributed.

00:12:06.010 --> 00:12:07.968
But in my time there were some computers,

00:12:07.968 --> 00:12:09.900
because we were engineering students,

00:12:09.900 --> 00:12:14.143
and one of my friends
actually ran a publication,

00:12:18.540 --> 00:12:19.373
was a publisher.

00:12:19.373 --> 00:12:23.230
So he helped us actually
make those pamphlets

00:12:23.230 --> 00:12:26.620
by typing into the computer
and just printing them out.

00:12:26.620 --> 00:12:27.770
So it was much easier for us.

00:12:27.770 --> 00:12:31.843
But earlier than our days,
it was much more difficult.

00:12:35.460 --> 00:12:37.930
- Let's turn now to Thawda Aye Lei.

00:12:37.930 --> 00:12:39.608
Tell us a little bit about your experience

00:12:39.608 --> 00:12:41.543
emerging as a writer.

00:12:42.460 --> 00:12:44.640
- Thank you for giving me a chance

00:12:44.640 --> 00:12:46.590
to share my passion story.

00:12:46.590 --> 00:12:51.395
Actually, I will say it
is because of my mother

00:12:51.395 --> 00:12:53.800
I became a writer,

00:12:53.800 --> 00:12:57.820
because she cultivated my
reading and writing practice

00:12:57.820 --> 00:12:59.403
since my childhood.

00:13:00.240 --> 00:13:02.131
Because when I was a child

00:13:02.131 --> 00:13:05.810
and it was around 10 years old,

00:13:05.810 --> 00:13:07.667
my mother give me a book,

00:13:07.667 --> 00:13:12.114
which is called "Zlata's Diary."

00:13:12.114 --> 00:13:16.150
Zlata is young girl living in Sarajevo,

00:13:16.150 --> 00:13:20.460
and she wrote about her
life during the Bosnian war

00:13:21.690 --> 00:13:23.172
from a childs perspective.

00:13:23.172 --> 00:13:25.880
So it's like the "Ann Frank Diary."

00:13:25.880 --> 00:13:30.690
So she became well known
after her book released,

00:13:30.690 --> 00:13:35.690
and finally the UN helped
her family to move to Paris.

00:13:36.010 --> 00:13:39.370
So after reading her diary,

00:13:39.370 --> 00:13:44.370
my mom told me if you want
to go to Paris like Zlata,

00:13:44.750 --> 00:13:47.840
you also need to keep a diary.

00:13:47.840 --> 00:13:52.840
So I started noting down all
my daily life into my diary.

00:13:54.470 --> 00:13:56.890
And another thing is,

00:13:56.890 --> 00:13:59.450
as my father is also a short story writer,

00:13:59.450 --> 00:14:03.823
he encouraged me to work as
a journalist for a newspaper.

00:14:05.405 --> 00:14:07.430
So after I was graduated

00:14:07.430 --> 00:14:09.664
from the University of Foreign Languages,

00:14:09.664 --> 00:14:11.113
I became a journalist.

00:14:12.040 --> 00:14:15.510
So the most challenging thing at the time

00:14:17.280 --> 00:14:22.280
it is that we didn't have
press freedom at the time.

00:14:22.410 --> 00:14:25.820
So yeah, censorship is the
most challenging thing.

00:14:25.820 --> 00:14:28.820
So back after 2008,

00:14:28.820 --> 00:14:33.260
we came to know that we can
create blog and website,

00:14:33.260 --> 00:14:38.090
and we can also post there for
anything where we wanted to.

00:14:38.090 --> 00:14:39.816
So around 2010,

00:14:39.816 --> 00:14:42.340
I published an online magazine,

00:14:42.340 --> 00:14:44.190
which is called "Myat shu."

00:14:44.190 --> 00:14:48.090
And then in 2017, yes,

00:14:48.090 --> 00:14:52.130
I published my first novel,
which is a print version.

00:14:52.130 --> 00:14:53.973
So to say frankly,

00:14:55.100 --> 00:14:58.440
whenever I write something,

00:14:58.440 --> 00:15:01.730
I feel that I am representing the group.

00:15:01.730 --> 00:15:06.300
So that's why writing is
one of my favorite things,

00:15:06.300 --> 00:15:09.126
because I feel that I
can represent the groups.

00:15:09.126 --> 00:15:13.963
And then, I prefer to read the books

00:15:13.963 --> 00:15:17.508
written by female authors, Virginia Woolf,

00:15:17.508 --> 00:15:18.464
Simone de Beauvoir

00:15:18.464 --> 00:15:21.130
and some other published female writers,

00:15:21.130 --> 00:15:26.130
as I love to know how
women approach the society

00:15:26.390 --> 00:15:29.830
from the female perspective
to literature, that's why.

00:15:29.830 --> 00:15:31.247
Yeah, thank you.

00:15:32.830 --> 00:15:35.120
- It's really interesting
what you were saying

00:15:35.120 --> 00:15:37.280
about the moments in which

00:15:37.280 --> 00:15:39.510
you were able to find the opportunity

00:15:39.510 --> 00:15:40.470
to start publishing.

00:15:40.470 --> 00:15:43.980
You mentioned 2010, 2017.

00:15:43.980 --> 00:15:46.290
Many of us here recognize the importance

00:15:46.290 --> 00:15:48.410
of that period of change.

00:15:48.410 --> 00:15:49.877
So I'm gonna circle back to you,

00:15:49.877 --> 00:15:52.729
'cause this is a topic I wanted to raise

00:15:52.729 --> 00:15:54.360
with Ko Ko Thett first,

00:15:54.360 --> 00:15:55.470
but then we'll come back to you.

00:15:55.470 --> 00:15:57.540
I want you to pick up on that.

00:15:57.540 --> 00:15:59.203
But the question I have.

00:15:59.203 --> 00:16:02.440
Among scholars and observers,

00:16:02.440 --> 00:16:07.440
2011 is often spoken of as
a kind of watershed year

00:16:07.740 --> 00:16:12.300
as an emergence of a kind of
new set of potential freedoms.

00:16:12.300 --> 00:16:15.240
But there's also been
talk in the scholarship

00:16:15.240 --> 00:16:17.430
and among observers that it was a kind of

00:16:17.430 --> 00:16:21.880
ephemeral moment as well
in the coup of 2021,

00:16:21.880 --> 00:16:26.880
has been a very dark shift
back to an alternative,

00:16:28.310 --> 00:16:31.310
more repressive approach to governance.

00:16:31.310 --> 00:16:34.670
And so what I wanted you to reflect upon

00:16:34.670 --> 00:16:37.510
is if you could tell me a little bit about

00:16:37.510 --> 00:16:39.110
the difference as a writer,

00:16:39.110 --> 00:16:44.110
what did it feel like to
be a writer before 2011,

00:16:44.420 --> 00:16:48.670
from 2011 to 2021, and then today?

00:16:48.670 --> 00:16:50.500
And to kind of give us a sense

00:16:50.500 --> 00:16:54.682
of the really dramatic
differences of those conditions,

00:16:54.682 --> 00:16:57.290
and what it meant to be a
writer during those periods?

00:16:57.290 --> 00:16:59.060
And Ko Ko Thett, I
wanted to start with you,

00:16:59.060 --> 00:17:00.580
'cause I know that you've been

00:17:00.580 --> 00:17:02.390
an itinerant writer in many ways,

00:17:02.390 --> 00:17:03.690
but there was a period in there

00:17:03.690 --> 00:17:05.900
where you had returned to Myanmar as well.

00:17:05.900 --> 00:17:07.890
And I thought you might
be able to start us off

00:17:07.890 --> 00:17:09.173
on that conversation.

00:17:10.710 --> 00:17:15.710
- Yes, before the decade of 2010s,

00:17:17.551 --> 00:17:20.930
the situation when I
was living in Myanmar,

00:17:20.930 --> 00:17:22.957
before I left Myanmar in the 1990s,

00:17:24.730 --> 00:17:26.263
the situation was abysmal.

00:17:27.550 --> 00:17:32.550
The decade started off with
the 1988 nationwide uprising

00:17:33.220 --> 00:17:35.030
and then 1990 election,

00:17:35.030 --> 00:17:38.930
where the opposition party
National League for Democracy

00:17:38.930 --> 00:17:39.820
won a landslide.

00:17:39.820 --> 00:17:43.407
So in that decade, dozens
of well known writers,

00:17:46.460 --> 00:17:48.430
including Ma Thida, Ma Thanegi,

00:17:48.430 --> 00:17:51.240
and so many others were jailed.

00:17:51.240 --> 00:17:53.780
So the situation was really abysmal.

00:17:53.780 --> 00:17:57.370
And to write freely means
to publish just illegally,

00:17:57.370 --> 00:17:58.203
which we did.

00:17:58.203 --> 00:18:01.830
So that was the situation before 2010.

00:18:01.830 --> 00:18:04.300
And in the 2010s,

00:18:04.300 --> 00:18:08.860
I was observing the
situation from a distance,

00:18:08.860 --> 00:18:11.220
but three things happened I'm Myanmar.

00:18:11.220 --> 00:18:13.640
The first thing was
introduction of the internet

00:18:13.640 --> 00:18:16.950
or social media and mobile devices.

00:18:16.950 --> 00:18:18.540
And the second thing was the removal

00:18:18.540 --> 00:18:22.120
of much of the censorship in 2012.

00:18:22.120 --> 00:18:24.300
And the third one was coming of age

00:18:24.300 --> 00:18:26.560
of the so-called digital generation.

00:18:26.560 --> 00:18:29.530
So these three things converged,

00:18:29.530 --> 00:18:31.790
and there was a sea
change in the landscape,

00:18:31.790 --> 00:18:33.320
literally landscape in Burma.

00:18:33.320 --> 00:18:38.320
So when I returned to Myanmar in 2012,

00:18:38.560 --> 00:18:41.088
after I left the country in 1997...

00:18:41.088 --> 00:18:45.910
And 15 years, it took me 15
years to return to Burma.

00:18:45.910 --> 00:18:48.140
And when I returned to
the country in 2012,

00:18:48.140 --> 00:18:50.060
I noticed there was a sea change

00:18:50.060 --> 00:18:52.450
in publication and literary landscape,

00:18:52.450 --> 00:18:55.363
as if the 1990s never existed.

00:18:56.870 --> 00:18:57.703
Having said that,

00:18:57.703 --> 00:19:01.540
of course, some writers
and poet were also jailed

00:19:01.540 --> 00:19:06.540
in the 2010s, and even
under the NLD government.

00:19:06.720 --> 00:19:11.060
So today, since the February coup,

00:19:11.060 --> 00:19:13.170
writers and poet continued to write,

00:19:13.170 --> 00:19:15.500
but as far as I know,

00:19:15.500 --> 00:19:18.640
no single writer or poet has been jailed

00:19:18.640 --> 00:19:20.743
or targeted for their writing.

00:19:22.324 --> 00:19:24.570
Writers and poets have
been jailed or murdered

00:19:24.570 --> 00:19:26.790
because they were leading the protests

00:19:26.790 --> 00:19:28.090
or they were part of the protests,

00:19:28.090 --> 00:19:31.463
but not a single writer had
been jailed for their writing.

00:19:33.230 --> 00:19:36.310
So in my opinion, it's more
like self censorship today

00:19:36.310 --> 00:19:40.713
than the repressive regime of censorship.

00:19:44.610 --> 00:19:46.010
- So Thawda Aye Lei,

00:19:46.010 --> 00:19:47.912
maybe you could return to
what you were describing

00:19:47.912 --> 00:19:51.330
about some of the transformation
in your own writing.

00:19:51.330 --> 00:19:53.963
So how did you experience those periods?

00:19:55.458 --> 00:20:00.458
- Yes, I would say after 2012,

00:20:00.540 --> 00:20:05.540
we enjoyed the right to
freedom of expressions.

00:20:06.380 --> 00:20:09.590
It's not a totally one,

00:20:09.590 --> 00:20:10.880
because I would say

00:20:10.880 --> 00:20:12.640
just a partial freedom,

00:20:12.640 --> 00:20:15.690
because the government,

00:20:15.690 --> 00:20:18.743
still wanted to push a
self censorship practice.

00:20:19.851 --> 00:20:24.610
It means that we did not
need to submit our writings

00:20:24.610 --> 00:20:29.610
to do their press as screening
board before publication,

00:20:30.050 --> 00:20:35.050
but we just needed to edit
our writings by ourself.

00:20:36.450 --> 00:20:40.100
That was not to be
against the existing law.

00:20:40.100 --> 00:20:42.040
But you know, it's not clear

00:20:43.730 --> 00:20:45.860
what kinds of things are supposed to be

00:20:45.860 --> 00:20:47.810
in violation of existing laws?

00:20:47.810 --> 00:20:51.213
That's why our life is
really risky, I will say.

00:20:53.312 --> 00:20:57.010
So it was like we could be
taken action at any time

00:20:57.010 --> 00:20:59.411
or prosecuted, or arrested,

00:20:59.411 --> 00:21:02.950
under the National
Security Law, Cyber law,

00:21:02.950 --> 00:21:05.310
or Telecommunication Law.

00:21:05.310 --> 00:21:10.310
But I would say as long as the dictators

00:21:10.760 --> 00:21:13.395
repression strategies are getting harsh,

00:21:13.395 --> 00:21:17.980
our defensive mechanism
has also been changed

00:21:17.980 --> 00:21:20.083
to respond to their repressions.

00:21:20.940 --> 00:21:25.180
What I would like to say
is those restrictions

00:21:26.240 --> 00:21:30.613
cannot stop our creation
and our flowing ideas.

00:21:33.142 --> 00:21:34.500
In the previous time,

00:21:34.500 --> 00:21:38.640
I mean initiating of
press executed involved,

00:21:38.640 --> 00:21:40.230
the writers didn't have a chance

00:21:40.230 --> 00:21:43.880
to talk about human rights abuse issues,

00:21:43.880 --> 00:21:46.980
or poverty or ethnic
conflicts or democracy

00:21:46.980 --> 00:21:48.460
through their writings,

00:21:48.460 --> 00:21:51.203
so they had to use some metaphors.

00:21:52.630 --> 00:21:56.210
That metaphor can reflect

00:21:56.210 --> 00:21:58.620
what they wanted to say in some ways,

00:21:58.620 --> 00:22:00.960
when they wanted to present a stories.

00:22:00.960 --> 00:22:05.850
So because at the time we had
only a print media platform

00:22:05.850 --> 00:22:08.610
that now the situation is changing

00:22:08.610 --> 00:22:12.110
as we have online platform as well.

00:22:12.110 --> 00:22:14.740
So the way all their
repressions were changed

00:22:14.740 --> 00:22:16.150
to digital repressions,

00:22:16.150 --> 00:22:19.610
along with the digital
authoritarian regime,

00:22:19.610 --> 00:22:23.480
then our resistance
strategies were also changed.

00:22:23.480 --> 00:22:26.373
So for the time being,

00:22:27.340 --> 00:22:29.840
the military restricted internet line,

00:22:29.840 --> 00:22:33.578
banned some website and
arrested some people

00:22:33.578 --> 00:22:35.090
for using the VPN.

00:22:35.090 --> 00:22:37.940
Although we had such kind
of challenges on our site,

00:22:37.940 --> 00:22:39.800
we published the online newsletter,

00:22:39.800 --> 00:22:42.270
which are more concerned
with the news article

00:22:42.270 --> 00:22:43.730
or other creative writings,

00:22:43.730 --> 00:22:46.970
about the current revolutions.

00:22:46.970 --> 00:22:51.447
And we did fundraising by
using the clip to donate sites.

00:22:51.447 --> 00:22:54.910
it means that when we visit our website

00:22:54.910 --> 00:22:58.050
and when we have a great
number of visitor and viewers

00:22:58.050 --> 00:23:02.130
who view the advertising on our channels,

00:23:02.130 --> 00:23:04.110
we will earn some money.

00:23:04.110 --> 00:23:08.500
So we post our stories or
audio books on our channels,

00:23:08.500 --> 00:23:12.090
then we invite people to
come and visit to our site,

00:23:12.090 --> 00:23:14.960
and read the story or
listen to the audiobooks.

00:23:14.960 --> 00:23:17.839
By doing that, we have two benefits.

00:23:17.839 --> 00:23:21.350
The first one is we can separate

00:23:21.350 --> 00:23:24.770
what we want to say to the
audience through our platform.

00:23:24.770 --> 00:23:28.450
And the second one is
we can make some income.

00:23:28.450 --> 00:23:31.110
So that income is now going into donations

00:23:31.110 --> 00:23:36.110
for those who joined these
civil disobedient movement

00:23:36.150 --> 00:23:38.060
and for the IDPs,

00:23:38.060 --> 00:23:41.030
and also for the human rights
defenders and protesters.

00:23:41.030 --> 00:23:44.410
So that is how we resist the
repression of the military

00:23:44.410 --> 00:23:48.870
on freedom of expression
by using the digital media.

00:23:48.870 --> 00:23:52.500
- Thawda Aye Lei, so what
you're describing now,

00:23:52.500 --> 00:23:56.830
these channels in which people can read

00:23:56.830 --> 00:23:58.300
and submit a donation

00:23:58.300 --> 00:24:01.760
for the privilege of reading the work,

00:24:01.760 --> 00:24:05.533
those are ongoing at the
moment right now in the moment?

00:24:06.682 --> 00:24:08.056
- Yeah.
- I wonder if maybe,

00:24:08.056 --> 00:24:10.410
when I'm talking to Ma Thida in a moment,

00:24:10.410 --> 00:24:12.280
if you could share a
link to some of those,

00:24:12.280 --> 00:24:15.709
I think that would be lovely
for our viewers to know about.

00:24:15.709 --> 00:24:16.560
- Oh, okay.

00:24:16.560 --> 00:24:19.180
- Are there any that are
in English language also?

00:24:19.180 --> 00:24:20.453
Not that it has to be.

00:24:21.900 --> 00:24:26.527
- No, actually the audio
books are just in Burmese.

00:24:28.190 --> 00:24:29.946
But yeah, I would share the link,

00:24:29.946 --> 00:24:31.986
if you are interested in-

00:24:31.986 --> 00:24:33.813
- Thank you, that would be wonderful.

00:24:35.180 --> 00:24:38.900
So Ma Thida, I end with you on this topic.

00:24:38.900 --> 00:24:40.570
How did you experience the difference

00:24:40.570 --> 00:24:45.433
between before, during and
after the 2010s period?

00:24:47.730 --> 00:24:48.563
- First of all,

00:24:48.563 --> 00:24:50.380
I just want to say a couple of writer

00:24:50.380 --> 00:24:52.560
has been arrested on the day of the coup.

00:24:52.560 --> 00:24:57.020
So even though we cannot
say the reasons behind,

00:24:57.020 --> 00:24:59.670
is thought to be because
of their literary works

00:24:59.670 --> 00:25:02.400
and literally related
activities, they were arrested,

00:25:02.400 --> 00:25:06.650
because it's was on the very
early morning of the coup,

00:25:06.650 --> 00:25:07.483
that's why.

00:25:08.880 --> 00:25:11.520
There are no more arrests

00:25:11.520 --> 00:25:15.260
or killing of the writers
or poets after the coup

00:25:15.260 --> 00:25:16.610
based on their works.

00:25:16.610 --> 00:25:19.960
But on the day of the coup,
we really don't know why,

00:25:19.960 --> 00:25:22.683
but they are a couple
writers like Maung Thar Cho,

00:25:24.380 --> 00:25:26.050
Htin Lin Oo, et cetera,

00:25:26.050 --> 00:25:29.140
So that's thought to their work

00:25:29.140 --> 00:25:32.820
somehow related to the arrest.

00:25:32.820 --> 00:25:37.150
So I think before 2012,

00:25:37.150 --> 00:25:41.265
before the censorship has been released,

00:25:41.265 --> 00:25:43.211
people struggle,

00:25:43.211 --> 00:25:45.500
but we need to be very careful

00:25:45.500 --> 00:25:49.270
about the period of the
censorship it's five decades.

00:25:49.270 --> 00:25:52.660
It's from 1962 to 2012.

00:25:52.660 --> 00:25:55.720
So for example, like some topics,

00:25:55.720 --> 00:26:00.720
land confiscation, or
criticisms against military,

00:26:02.050 --> 00:26:06.770
it's turned out be a taboo
topics for every people,

00:26:06.770 --> 00:26:11.660
not just to the writers.

00:26:11.660 --> 00:26:14.720
For that reason, I think
the self censorship

00:26:15.750 --> 00:26:19.000
it's not being noticed as self censorship,

00:26:19.000 --> 00:26:22.483
it's like a very standard normal practice.

00:26:23.630 --> 00:26:27.810
The writers, the poets
might write about something,

00:26:27.810 --> 00:26:30.370
they might be criticize,

00:26:30.370 --> 00:26:32.650
they might be pointing out the bribery

00:26:32.650 --> 00:26:34.730
or corruption, et cetera,

00:26:34.730 --> 00:26:39.213
but they never ever think
so much harsh criticisms

00:26:39.213 --> 00:26:41.450
against the military,

00:26:41.450 --> 00:26:44.640
because it's like for them,

00:26:44.640 --> 00:26:46.600
it is the normal standard,

00:26:46.600 --> 00:26:47.990
not go beyond this line.

00:26:47.990 --> 00:26:52.990
So of course it's a kind of
the unnoticed self censorship,

00:26:55.160 --> 00:26:57.441
but at the same time,

00:26:57.441 --> 00:26:59.560
kind of the peer censorship

00:26:59.560 --> 00:27:03.600
is more on the racial or
religious sensitivity.

00:27:03.600 --> 00:27:07.690
So it's, the writers tend not to write

00:27:07.690 --> 00:27:11.430
something racially or
religiously sensitive topics,

00:27:11.430 --> 00:27:14.970
because there will be no
censorship from the state,

00:27:14.970 --> 00:27:17.690
but from the peer groups.

00:27:17.690 --> 00:27:21.230
So they might get so much pressure,

00:27:21.230 --> 00:27:23.015
that's why not many writers

00:27:23.015 --> 00:27:27.580
are willing to cross this
line too, something like that.

00:27:27.580 --> 00:27:32.580
So I think after the 2012
relaxation of the censorship

00:27:33.330 --> 00:27:36.163
ending the press scrutiny board,

00:27:37.160 --> 00:27:42.160
we do have much more chance
to be independent, to be free,

00:27:42.530 --> 00:27:45.870
but it's also depending on
the either peer censorship

00:27:45.870 --> 00:27:48.890
or the self censorship,

00:27:48.890 --> 00:27:53.000
plus there are still
so many laws existing,

00:27:53.000 --> 00:27:56.100
pretty much prohibiting
the freedom expression.

00:27:56.100 --> 00:27:57.950
That's we have been working on.

00:27:58.960 --> 00:28:02.060
But good thing is after 2012,

00:28:02.060 --> 00:28:04.780
because of the Freedom of Association Law,

00:28:04.780 --> 00:28:05.970
Freedom Association Act,

00:28:05.970 --> 00:28:09.530
there are so many new organizations,

00:28:09.530 --> 00:28:11.800
new institution has been game up,

00:28:11.800 --> 00:28:13.547
for example, like PEN Myanmar.

00:28:13.547 --> 00:28:17.760
So PEN Myanmar set up in 2013,

00:28:17.760 --> 00:28:21.790
and then we have been
working mainly focused

00:28:21.790 --> 00:28:22.970
on the freedom expression,

00:28:22.970 --> 00:28:24.840
not on the literature.

00:28:24.840 --> 00:28:28.600
But we did have a survey in 2018,

00:28:28.600 --> 00:28:33.600
and it's for a decade from 2008 to 2018.

00:28:34.710 --> 00:28:38.550
We have been reading more
than 3000 short stories

00:28:38.550 --> 00:28:41.440
published in three well
known literary magazines

00:28:41.440 --> 00:28:46.440
to find how much the Myanmar
literature is inclusive

00:28:46.780 --> 00:28:49.960
in terms of gender, inclusive ethnicity,

00:28:49.960 --> 00:28:53.810
in terms of the demography,
something like that.

00:28:53.810 --> 00:28:57.020
And also we have been surveying about

00:28:57.020 --> 00:29:02.020
what's the difference
between the topics choosing

00:29:04.688 --> 00:29:07.120
for these stories.

00:29:07.120 --> 00:29:10.140
What we founded out is in terms of topics,

00:29:10.140 --> 00:29:11.095
it's not much change

00:29:11.095 --> 00:29:15.000
before the censorship
and after the censorship,

00:29:15.000 --> 00:29:18.330
that's mean a couple of new topics

00:29:18.330 --> 00:29:23.070
are becoming more presentable
at the literary magazines,

00:29:23.070 --> 00:29:27.920
for example, like love stories
of the LGBTQ community,

00:29:27.920 --> 00:29:28.982
something like that.

00:29:28.982 --> 00:29:32.610
And a lot of the topics like

00:29:32.610 --> 00:29:36.200
bribery and corruption in the past,

00:29:36.200 --> 00:29:38.290
but not many finding writing

00:29:39.680 --> 00:29:42.330
about the land confiscation by army

00:29:42.330 --> 00:29:46.653
or the other sufferings
in the ethnic groups.

00:29:47.600 --> 00:29:50.880
That's, I think the
education system itself

00:29:50.880 --> 00:29:55.610
it's pretty much the
official language is Burmese,

00:29:55.610 --> 00:29:58.570
so that's why the language plays a role,

00:29:58.570 --> 00:30:03.570
that's why not many ethnic
minority becoming the writers.

00:30:05.740 --> 00:30:07.240
If they become the writers,

00:30:07.240 --> 00:30:11.570
they cannot use their own mother tongue.

00:30:11.570 --> 00:30:15.960
Only the Burmese language
is the medium for us.

00:30:15.960 --> 00:30:20.110
So that's why I think
when we talk about this,

00:30:20.110 --> 00:30:25.110
it's like not very positive
in terms of freedom

00:30:25.506 --> 00:30:28.910
after the censorship has terminated

00:30:28.910 --> 00:30:30.300
during the NLD government.

00:30:30.300 --> 00:30:35.300
But I have been watching
some movie clips in the past

00:30:35.680 --> 00:30:38.560
before the 2020 general election.

00:30:38.560 --> 00:30:40.930
And that movie clips

00:30:40.930 --> 00:30:45.610
is the kind of the documentary
interviewing general public

00:30:45.610 --> 00:30:48.750
in so many different
cities inside the country.

00:30:48.750 --> 00:30:52.580
And a lot of general
public, both men and women,

00:30:52.580 --> 00:30:54.860
laymen, and the intellectuals,

00:30:54.860 --> 00:30:59.220
they mentioned that during
the last five years,

00:30:59.220 --> 00:31:03.230
they feel pretty safer to criticize

00:31:03.230 --> 00:31:05.200
their own elected MPS,

00:31:05.200 --> 00:31:07.473
and the regional
governments cabinet members,

00:31:08.420 --> 00:31:09.350
something like that.

00:31:09.350 --> 00:31:13.890
So maybe the incidence of
the number of people arrested

00:31:13.890 --> 00:31:17.170
because it spreads freely,

00:31:17.170 --> 00:31:21.490
it's a little bit higher
during the last five years

00:31:21.490 --> 00:31:23.280
compared to the U then San government,

00:31:23.280 --> 00:31:28.280
but we don't really have that
kind of the detail survey,

00:31:29.530 --> 00:31:31.283
but it's thought to be,

00:31:31.283 --> 00:31:34.050
more people dare enough

00:31:34.050 --> 00:31:36.860
to practice their freedom expressions

00:31:36.860 --> 00:31:38.630
compared to the then San's government.

00:31:38.630 --> 00:31:40.500
That's also, we need to recognize.

00:31:40.500 --> 00:31:42.930
So that's why I think the whole picture

00:31:42.930 --> 00:31:44.910
is pretty much complicated.

00:31:44.910 --> 00:31:49.340
I cannot say black and
white in that sense.

00:31:49.340 --> 00:31:52.145
So I think a good thing is

00:31:52.145 --> 00:31:56.260
there are a couple of the writers based

00:31:56.260 --> 00:32:00.270
or literature based
organizations like PEN Myanmar,

00:32:00.270 --> 00:32:03.130
is more focused on freedom expression,

00:32:03.130 --> 00:32:06.080
but the other organization focused mainly

00:32:06.080 --> 00:32:07.870
on writers and poets.

00:32:07.870 --> 00:32:11.163
There are so many came up before the coup.

00:32:14.560 --> 00:32:16.410
- Thank you so much for sharing this.

00:32:17.770 --> 00:32:21.459
At this moment, before we turn to open up

00:32:21.459 --> 00:32:23.760
a more open discussion,

00:32:23.760 --> 00:32:27.570
I want to invite each of you
to share piece of your writing

00:32:27.570 --> 00:32:28.873
with the group.

00:32:28.873 --> 00:32:32.623
A lot of us are really eager
to hear some of your work.

00:32:33.550 --> 00:32:36.630
So I've asked you to
choose a short passage

00:32:36.630 --> 00:32:38.080
just to give us a taste.

00:32:38.080 --> 00:32:41.123
And if you can just
tell us where it's from,

00:32:43.160 --> 00:32:44.500
why you chose the piece?

00:32:44.500 --> 00:32:46.870
And then just read it to us.

00:32:46.870 --> 00:32:48.928
I think we we'll start with Ko Ko Thett,

00:32:48.928 --> 00:32:51.840
then we'll turn to Thawda Aye Lei,

00:32:51.840 --> 00:32:54.200
and then Ma Thida at the end.

00:32:54.200 --> 00:32:56.640
And then I'll ask all of
you to reflect a little bit

00:32:56.640 --> 00:32:58.620
on each other's writing for a moment,

00:32:58.620 --> 00:33:02.030
and then we will open it
up to a group discussion

00:33:02.030 --> 00:33:05.000
with the audience as well,
for question and answer.

00:33:05.000 --> 00:33:07.800
So Ko Ko Thett, if you could
start, that'd be wonderful.

00:33:09.835 --> 00:33:11.210
- Thank you, Erik.

00:33:11.210 --> 00:33:12.280
I would like to read

00:33:14.550 --> 00:33:18.420
a poem from this
collection "Bamboophobia."

00:33:18.420 --> 00:33:22.963
And this collection was
written before 2021, actually,

00:33:25.436 --> 00:33:30.430
and was supposed to be
published before that,

00:33:30.430 --> 00:33:33.312
but then there was some
delay and it's just out.

00:33:33.312 --> 00:33:37.937
So I'm going to read a
poem called "Celebrations,"

00:33:38.980 --> 00:33:43.637
and from this "Bamboophobia."

00:33:50.650 --> 00:33:51.973
Celebrations,

00:33:54.100 --> 00:33:59.100
over here, we celebrate
absolutely everything,

00:33:59.400 --> 00:34:01.303
every single day.

00:34:03.440 --> 00:34:07.573
Every day is someone's
birthday or funeral.

00:34:09.120 --> 00:34:14.120
Every other day is an anniversary
of a massacre or wedding,

00:34:17.020 --> 00:34:19.920
we celebrate every week

00:34:19.920 --> 00:34:22.650
when delouse ourselves

00:34:22.650 --> 00:34:27.253
by rubbing our shaven heads with lime.

00:34:28.940 --> 00:34:33.940
Every three months we deworm
our stomachs with papaya seeds.

00:34:35.760 --> 00:34:40.760
We celebrate the spiritual
power of our polluted rivers.

00:34:42.500 --> 00:34:45.693
We celebrate plastic in our air.

00:34:47.320 --> 00:34:51.983
We celebrate the lives of
spiders by frying them for food.

00:34:53.370 --> 00:34:57.573
We celebrate snakes by
skinning them into jerky.

00:34:58.490 --> 00:35:01.530
Sex real or imagined

00:35:02.370 --> 00:35:05.973
is the celebration of our bodies arousal.

00:35:07.580 --> 00:35:12.393
Orgasm is a celebration of
bodies contented closure.

00:35:13.420 --> 00:35:15.513
We celebrate celibacy.

00:35:16.410 --> 00:35:21.113
We celebrate and are humbled
when rapist apologize.

00:35:22.180 --> 00:35:24.860
We feast with plenty of arak

00:35:24.860 --> 00:35:27.720
when a mother manages to rent a womb

00:35:28.640 --> 00:35:30.083
or sell her child.

00:35:31.430 --> 00:35:34.930
On the occasions of the funerals of poets

00:35:34.930 --> 00:35:38.030
who died from excessive drinking,

00:35:38.030 --> 00:35:42.753
we toast rice wine over their
graves and get this drunk.

00:35:44.470 --> 00:35:46.530
When there is nothing to celebrate,

00:35:46.530 --> 00:35:49.763
we celebrate for nothing with nothing.

00:35:50.900 --> 00:35:55.000
All day long, every
single day of the year,

00:35:55.000 --> 00:35:58.077
we smile and say to each other

00:35:58.077 --> 00:36:00.476
(speaking in foreign language)

00:36:00.476 --> 00:36:01.560
how are you today?

00:36:01.560 --> 00:36:02.623
Have you eaten?

00:36:03.756 --> 00:36:05.120
And (speaking in foreign language)

00:36:05.120 --> 00:36:06.853
sleep well before bed.

00:36:07.730 --> 00:36:11.470
Come morning we say,
(speaking in foreign language)

00:36:11.470 --> 00:36:12.380
have you eaten,

00:36:12.380 --> 00:36:14.073
to celebrate the day,

00:36:14.940 --> 00:36:17.883
for we are still here.

00:36:26.410 --> 00:36:27.243
Thank you.

00:36:28.590 --> 00:36:29.840
- Thank you, Ko Ko Thett.

00:36:31.260 --> 00:36:32.360
Thawda Aye Lei,

00:36:32.360 --> 00:36:34.460
would you like to share a passage with us?

00:36:35.460 --> 00:36:38.480
- Yes, I would like to share some pieces

00:36:38.480 --> 00:36:43.250
from my short story
"The Heat of Laughter,"

00:36:43.250 --> 00:36:47.700
because for me, I am
personally more interested

00:36:47.700 --> 00:36:50.620
in telling the way survival of people

00:36:51.720 --> 00:36:53.787
among the chaos conditions,

00:36:53.787 --> 00:36:56.380
because I would like to empower

00:36:56.380 --> 00:36:57.880
and increase their resiliency,

00:36:59.009 --> 00:37:02.110
while are resisting the repressions.

00:37:02.110 --> 00:37:06.700
So that's why in my short story,
"In The Heat of Laughter,"

00:37:06.700 --> 00:37:10.860
I wrote about the sense of
humor of Burmese people,

00:37:10.860 --> 00:37:13.020
even in the worst situation,

00:37:13.020 --> 00:37:18.020
because it is easy to make as a set

00:37:18.230 --> 00:37:23.230
or obsess as our world
is full of chaos things.

00:37:24.260 --> 00:37:27.920
However, it is not easy to make a smile.

00:37:27.920 --> 00:37:32.040
So if we can overcome such bitterness

00:37:32.040 --> 00:37:35.840
and can make an irony towards those chaos,

00:37:35.840 --> 00:37:38.390
I will say, is a kind of something

00:37:38.390 --> 00:37:42.041
that we are free from all anxieties.

00:37:42.041 --> 00:37:44.889
And I feel that the feeling of our spirit

00:37:44.889 --> 00:37:47.470
enrich through the extreme part,

00:37:47.470 --> 00:37:49.190
that's why I would like to highlight

00:37:49.190 --> 00:37:53.620
the sense of humor of
Burmese people in my story.

00:37:53.620 --> 00:37:56.123
So let me read some pieces.

00:37:58.590 --> 00:38:00.530
The main character, Kyaw Kyaw

00:38:00.530 --> 00:38:03.573
he is optimistic about
the current situation.

00:38:04.657 --> 00:38:06.497
"Kyaw Kyaw was lucky.

00:38:06.497 --> 00:38:10.157
"He was taken near the
start of the protests.

00:38:10.157 --> 00:38:14.717
"Later, the monsters
were let loose at night.

00:38:14.717 --> 00:38:17.067
"Criminals roamed the streets.

00:38:17.067 --> 00:38:19.013
"Houses were set on fire.

00:38:20.207 --> 00:38:22.087
"Curfews were imposed.

00:38:22.087 --> 00:38:25.457
"Each day was worse than the one before,

00:38:25.457 --> 00:38:28.507
"and all I could do was cry, curse,

00:38:28.507 --> 00:38:30.910
"and click my tongue in bitterness.

00:38:30.910 --> 00:38:33.647
"Kyaw Kyaw never changed though.

00:38:33.647 --> 00:38:36.117
"After our leaders were taken,

00:38:36.117 --> 00:38:40.977
"we marched in protest
for miles across the city.

00:38:40.977 --> 00:38:43.217
"My belly is too fat anyway.

00:38:43.217 --> 00:38:46.127
"It's about time I got some exercise.

00:38:46.127 --> 00:38:49.533
"After Facebook and
other sites were blocked,

00:38:49.533 --> 00:38:53.840
"we all turned to VPNs
and secure messaging apps.

00:38:55.666 --> 00:38:59.787
"Before, I didn't know
anything about this technology.

00:38:59.787 --> 00:39:02.597
"Now, I'm like an expert.

00:39:02.597 --> 00:39:04.947
"After the state media announced

00:39:04.947 --> 00:39:08.127
"a new raft of rules and regulations,

00:39:08.127 --> 00:39:11.537
"we could be arrested
for any little thing.

00:39:11.537 --> 00:39:13.627
"I'm thinking of becoming a lawyer

00:39:13.627 --> 00:39:17.647
"with all the new laws I'm learning about.

00:39:17.647 --> 00:39:19.437
"After the internet was cut

00:39:19.437 --> 00:39:23.527
"between 1:00 am and 9:00 am every night,

00:39:23.527 --> 00:39:26.317
"we went dark to the world.

00:39:26.317 --> 00:39:30.497
"Good, now I won't stay up
so late watching videos.

00:39:30.497 --> 00:39:33.967
"I can get back to my
normal sleep routine.

00:39:33.967 --> 00:39:38.727
"After civilians were
chased, beaten, and shot,

00:39:38.727 --> 00:39:41.767
"we built defense walls
across the streets.

00:39:41.767 --> 00:39:44.490
"If this is a game, we have leveled up."

00:39:44.490 --> 00:39:47.310
This is how Kyaw Kyaw earlier optimistic

00:39:47.310 --> 00:39:52.310
about the current situation
with his sense of humor.

00:39:52.610 --> 00:39:54.850
But here I forgot to mention one thing.

00:39:54.850 --> 00:39:57.990
There is one military force shot their gun

00:39:57.990 --> 00:40:00.233
at midnight in Yangon last year, February,

00:40:01.680 --> 00:40:04.800
how our people respond to those gang shots

00:40:04.800 --> 00:40:06.940
was really amazing.

00:40:06.940 --> 00:40:09.820
At the time the military shot the gun,

00:40:09.820 --> 00:40:12.770
as to threaten and to
make people frightened,

00:40:12.770 --> 00:40:14.740
while they were sleeping.

00:40:14.740 --> 00:40:17.310
That at the time we turned off the light,

00:40:17.310 --> 00:40:20.480
and then every time we heard the gunshot,

00:40:20.480 --> 00:40:23.230
we shouted "Happy New Year."

00:40:23.230 --> 00:40:27.130
So our voice yelling, "Happy New Year,"

00:40:27.130 --> 00:40:30.270
hugely covered the gunshot.

00:40:30.270 --> 00:40:34.620
That is one of the ways we
responded to the military threat.

00:40:34.620 --> 00:40:37.763
And I think that is truly unique,

00:40:38.970 --> 00:40:42.730
but I forgot to include
that one in my story.

00:40:42.730 --> 00:40:45.003
But other ways for
resistance are mentioned.

00:40:50.610 --> 00:40:51.693
- Thank you, Aye Lei.

00:40:53.520 --> 00:40:56.033
Ma Thida, would you
like to share a passage?

00:40:59.900 --> 00:41:03.310
- Yes, this piece I was...

00:41:03.310 --> 00:41:06.860
I wrote this piece after
the Saffron revolution

00:41:06.860 --> 00:41:10.760
and I think this will
be a good one to choose.

00:41:10.760 --> 00:41:12.880
But I couldn't read it is quite long.

00:41:12.880 --> 00:41:17.880
So, this is the story
called "Living the Horror."

00:41:17.960 --> 00:41:19.590
But in this piece,

00:41:19.590 --> 00:41:24.590
I make the writer looking
for a piece of writing

00:41:25.590 --> 00:41:30.520
and writing his O piece
and trying to edit it.

00:41:30.520 --> 00:41:32.250
So I'm going to read

00:41:32.250 --> 00:41:35.760
not just about the
narratives of the writer,

00:41:35.760 --> 00:41:40.760
but about its own edited
pieces of the writing,

00:41:46.039 --> 00:41:49.890
a bit of this, and then
the end of the story.

00:41:49.890 --> 00:41:52.227
So here it is.

00:41:52.227 --> 00:41:54.807
"Bang, Bang two gunshots.

00:41:54.807 --> 00:41:56.593
"This is not a Hollywood studio,

00:41:57.747 --> 00:42:01.677
"It is just a main
avenue in a Burmese city.

00:42:01.677 --> 00:42:04.677
"A road peacefully used by public servant,

00:42:04.677 --> 00:42:07.547
"civilians, students, teachers,

00:42:07.547 --> 00:42:10.637
"street vendors, monks and nuns.

00:42:10.637 --> 00:42:13.177
"A road throbbing with noise cars,

00:42:13.177 --> 00:42:15.130
"chatting, blaring, music,

00:42:15.130 --> 00:42:19.937
"birds, pigeons, and three ducks.

00:42:19.937 --> 00:42:23.737
"But these two gunshots
have transformed it.

00:42:23.737 --> 00:42:26.607
"Now you cannot see anybody on the avenue.

00:42:26.607 --> 00:42:28.537
"Instead of steps,

00:42:28.537 --> 00:42:32.477
"they are just scattered
sandals left behind.

00:42:32.477 --> 00:42:37.157
"Not even a pigeon or a dog can be heard,

00:42:37.157 --> 00:42:39.977
"let alone people's voices.

00:42:39.977 --> 00:42:44.977
"It's just a far noon, but
the sky is gray and dirty,

00:42:45.337 --> 00:42:47.397
"already threatening rain.

00:42:47.397 --> 00:42:51.887
"The sense of food and fruits
from the street vendors

00:42:51.887 --> 00:42:56.887
"have vanished, replaced by
smells of smoke and bullets.

00:42:57.227 --> 00:43:01.757
"Avoiding smokey corners
the steps are wavering,

00:43:01.757 --> 00:43:06.757
"and ash to glaze backward
is slowing down progress.

00:43:06.947 --> 00:43:08.717
"While looking backward,

00:43:08.717 --> 00:43:13.477
"the forward moving body is
afraid it might get pierced

00:43:13.477 --> 00:43:16.517
"by something at the front.

00:43:16.517 --> 00:43:21.017
"That thought hastens
the steps once again."

00:43:22.360 --> 00:43:24.293
And another piece.

00:43:25.337 --> 00:43:27.267
"That voice is similar.

00:43:27.267 --> 00:43:32.267
"Yes, I heard that very
wise, not along ago,

00:43:32.647 --> 00:43:34.757
"but then I felt safe.

00:43:34.757 --> 00:43:36.987
"Now I feel the opposite.

00:43:36.987 --> 00:43:38.837
"Still right now,

00:43:38.837 --> 00:43:41.877
"I will make myself face anything,

00:43:41.877 --> 00:43:44.987
"if it means getting a story.

00:43:44.987 --> 00:43:48.467
"So tell me what sort of story you have.

00:43:48.467 --> 00:43:50.747
"My voice sounds dry,

00:43:50.747 --> 00:43:52.167
"but the relief of finding,

00:43:52.167 --> 00:43:55.587
"I am able to master audible speech,

00:43:55.587 --> 00:43:58.063
"steadies my trembling body.

00:43:59.057 --> 00:44:00.240
"I am...

00:44:01.277 --> 00:44:03.297
"But then that familiar voice

00:44:03.297 --> 00:44:06.527
"simply fades away from my ears.

00:44:06.527 --> 00:44:11.527
"That self formed body drift
back into the darkness,

00:44:11.957 --> 00:44:16.957
"along with his horrid laughing face.

00:44:17.087 --> 00:44:19.527
"The big hole on his chest,

00:44:19.527 --> 00:44:23.027
"floats away like a flying saucer,

00:44:23.027 --> 00:44:25.317
"moving into darkness.

00:44:25.317 --> 00:44:27.547
"Strongly through the smell of blood

00:44:27.547 --> 00:44:31.077
"remains as strong as ever.

00:44:31.077 --> 00:44:34.617
"At this moment another
wave of white smoke

00:44:34.617 --> 00:44:37.227
"rises up like a tide.

00:44:37.227 --> 00:44:40.067
"It does not occur to me to ask

00:44:40.067 --> 00:44:43.297
"why an avenue in the middle of this city

00:44:43.297 --> 00:44:47.157
"has become a channel for smoke waves

00:44:47.157 --> 00:44:51.807
"My whole attention is
focused on trying to discern

00:44:51.807 --> 00:44:54.277
"what it is that I can see

00:44:54.277 --> 00:44:57.747
"moving towards me in the smoke.

00:44:57.747 --> 00:45:01.407
"What shapes are gradually emerging."

00:45:02.780 --> 00:45:06.927
So I'm going to read the last passage.

00:45:06.927 --> 00:45:10.827
"The images that are
deep inside the nozzle

00:45:10.827 --> 00:45:14.607
"are gradually approaching my chest.

00:45:14.607 --> 00:45:16.477
"I can see them now.

00:45:16.477 --> 00:45:18.943
"I even know what they are,

00:45:19.877 --> 00:45:24.877
"but I don't have the capacity
to define them, not yet.

00:45:25.407 --> 00:45:27.657
"I embrace the fear.

00:45:27.657 --> 00:45:31.247
"I try to embrace the horror,

00:45:31.247 --> 00:45:32.584
"even the kind of horror

00:45:32.584 --> 00:45:36.617
"that is so popular with the masses.

00:45:36.617 --> 00:45:41.417
"But ultimately, I don't have the courage

00:45:41.417 --> 00:45:45.227
"to express that kind of horror.

00:45:45.227 --> 00:45:50.227
"The images are still
approaching towards my chest.

00:45:51.616 --> 00:45:54.057
"'Stop, please stop,'

00:45:54.057 --> 00:45:59.057
"I scream in silence and shut my eyes.

00:45:59.087 --> 00:46:02.327
"Enough, I have no longer wish

00:46:02.327 --> 00:46:04.873
"to continue writing this story.

00:46:06.587 --> 00:46:10.527
"Please respect the courage
it took to make this request

00:46:10.527 --> 00:46:13.110
"and let me leave the horror."

00:46:13.110 --> 00:46:13.943
Thank you.

00:46:20.200 --> 00:46:21.323
- Thank you so much.

00:46:23.860 --> 00:46:24.860
What I'd like to do,

00:46:24.860 --> 00:46:27.050
is ask the three of you

00:46:27.050 --> 00:46:30.860
to just reflect on each
other's passages for a moment,

00:46:30.860 --> 00:46:32.660
and then we will open it up

00:46:32.660 --> 00:46:36.460
to an open question and
answer with the group here.

00:46:36.460 --> 00:46:39.860
I just, I'm curious to understand

00:46:39.860 --> 00:46:42.130
how you receive each other's writings

00:46:42.130 --> 00:46:44.360
and some of the thoughts that emerge

00:46:44.360 --> 00:46:45.860
as you hear each other's work.

00:46:49.410 --> 00:46:50.980
Maybe I'll start with Ko Ko Thett

00:46:50.980 --> 00:46:53.083
with some reflections on the other two.

00:46:55.470 --> 00:46:56.303
- Yes.

00:47:01.000 --> 00:47:05.240
Thawda Aye Lei, I like
her writing and essays.

00:47:07.366 --> 00:47:08.866
One of our essays,

00:47:10.697 --> 00:47:13.287
"Whose Footfall is Loudest,"

00:47:14.190 --> 00:47:19.080
is about the importance
of footwear, flip flops

00:47:20.430 --> 00:47:25.143
in the revolution since
the 1962, 1988, 2021.

00:47:26.330 --> 00:47:31.330
And so after a shootout,
military shootout,

00:47:31.700 --> 00:47:34.830
people usually flee and always left,

00:47:34.830 --> 00:47:37.730
often in the streets could be flip flops.

00:47:37.730 --> 00:47:39.080
So she wrote about that

00:47:40.670 --> 00:47:43.010
touchingly, poignantly.

00:47:43.010 --> 00:47:48.010
And she is very good at
portraying mundane subjects

00:47:48.440 --> 00:47:51.750
and people in her stories.

00:47:51.750 --> 00:47:54.200
So my question is quite simple.

00:47:54.200 --> 00:47:56.830
What were you reading recently,

00:47:56.830 --> 00:48:01.830
and what was your favorite
book in Burmese or English,

00:48:02.210 --> 00:48:03.943
fiction on nonfiction?

00:48:05.583 --> 00:48:10.236
- Yeah, actually there
are many books I like,

00:48:10.236 --> 00:48:12.803
so it is difficult to mention only one.

00:48:13.819 --> 00:48:17.773
But recently, there are
some book I have read,

00:48:20.020 --> 00:48:21.840
about those big books,

00:48:21.840 --> 00:48:25.310
and I don't wanna share
here is a little bit boring.

00:48:25.310 --> 00:48:30.310
So one of the book I have
recently read is, "Educated,"

00:48:34.280 --> 00:48:37.083
which is written by Tara Westover.

00:48:38.520 --> 00:48:40.670
Actually is like a memoir.

00:48:40.670 --> 00:48:45.123
She wrote about herself and her family,

00:48:46.722 --> 00:48:50.703
how she survived under
her discipline father,

00:48:52.900 --> 00:48:54.180
he was too religious,

00:48:54.180 --> 00:48:59.045
and how she escaped from
the stress of her brothers

00:48:59.045 --> 00:49:02.260
who has a very abusive nature.

00:49:02.260 --> 00:49:06.760
And even though her family
support her education,

00:49:06.760 --> 00:49:11.760
she tell her story how she
trying to excel to education

00:49:13.970 --> 00:49:17.480
and how she struggled to obtain PhD.

00:49:17.480 --> 00:49:20.240
However, I think the
book is more than that,

00:49:20.240 --> 00:49:24.260
because I feel that not
only about the education,

00:49:24.260 --> 00:49:27.887
she really revealing about her way,

00:49:29.925 --> 00:49:34.925
of how she deconstructs the
religious norm and gender norms,

00:49:35.260 --> 00:49:37.380
she was shaped out for many years.

00:49:37.380 --> 00:49:40.440
I think this is really
important from her story.

00:49:40.440 --> 00:49:42.633
So it's really inspires to me.

00:49:48.000 --> 00:49:50.970
- And yeah, I have a
question for Ma Thida too.

00:49:50.970 --> 00:49:52.020
The question is that,

00:49:52.020 --> 00:49:56.100
you've been traveling the
length and the breadth

00:49:56.100 --> 00:50:00.010
and the depth of Burma
since all over places,

00:50:00.010 --> 00:50:01.217
all over the places in Myanmar

00:50:01.217 --> 00:50:02.860
Burma and Myanmar, since 1989,

00:50:02.860 --> 00:50:05.573
you were part of the NLD campaign,

00:50:07.300 --> 00:50:11.510
and you were traveling a lot
on that campaign in 1989.

00:50:11.510 --> 00:50:13.360
And then by 2019,

00:50:13.360 --> 00:50:15.220
you were also heading PEN Myanmar,

00:50:15.220 --> 00:50:16.627
and traveling all over the places.

00:50:16.627 --> 00:50:20.190
So which city or town Myanmar

00:50:20.190 --> 00:50:25.117
will be very friendly or
receptive to literary personnel?

00:50:26.130 --> 00:50:30.593
And what's the difference
from 1989 to 2019?

00:50:32.240 --> 00:50:35.300
- It's hard to say because I
have been to so many places,

00:50:35.300 --> 00:50:38.150
and what I remember is,

00:50:38.150 --> 00:50:39.913
according to my experience,

00:50:42.400 --> 00:50:45.830
appreciation for writers and poets

00:50:45.830 --> 00:50:49.820
are mainly from the middle of
the country, Burmese majority,

00:50:49.820 --> 00:50:51.940
because the language matters.

00:50:51.940 --> 00:50:55.810
They can read our piece very well,

00:50:55.810 --> 00:50:58.230
there will be no language
barrier for them.

00:50:58.230 --> 00:51:01.780
So I think I might say the Pakokku

00:51:01.780 --> 00:51:06.780
was one of the most
interesting during the 1988,

00:51:08.010 --> 00:51:09.960
according to my experience,

00:51:09.960 --> 00:51:14.640
during our visit to the
upper Burma, they show up.

00:51:14.640 --> 00:51:18.980
And then you might notice the
very beginning of the program

00:51:18.980 --> 00:51:21.730
or the Saffron Revolution
is also from Pakokku.

00:51:21.730 --> 00:51:26.180
And throughout the days after 2012,

00:51:26.180 --> 00:51:28.100
literally activities

00:51:29.460 --> 00:51:33.920
has been pretty much
very alive in Pakokku.

00:51:33.920 --> 00:51:35.810
So maybe I might say that...

00:51:35.810 --> 00:51:38.880
But there are so many other cities still,

00:51:38.880 --> 00:51:40.300
I cannot just name it.

00:51:40.300 --> 00:51:43.576
There are so many, so many other cities,

00:51:43.576 --> 00:51:46.760
very familiar with the...

00:51:47.850 --> 00:51:49.520
Some cities I might say

00:51:49.520 --> 00:51:53.083
in Ayeyarwady Division like Hinthada.

00:51:54.027 --> 00:51:57.010
Hinthada is famous for having

00:51:57.010 --> 00:52:02.010
at least three, four
literary activities per year.

00:52:02.580 --> 00:52:05.950
For the other cities
they might do only once,

00:52:05.950 --> 00:52:08.300
but for Hinthada it's might
be at least four, five,

00:52:08.300 --> 00:52:09.441
something like that.

00:52:09.441 --> 00:52:13.890
The poets in Hinthada
are very, very strong.

00:52:13.890 --> 00:52:17.830
So poets have their own
choice of the literary talk,

00:52:17.830 --> 00:52:20.310
and the other group also having their own,

00:52:20.310 --> 00:52:21.143
something like that.

00:52:21.143 --> 00:52:26.010
That are so many cities
are going on like this.

00:52:31.255 --> 00:52:32.088
- Thawda Aye Lei,

00:52:32.088 --> 00:52:34.480
would you like to pose
any questions to Ma Thida

00:52:34.480 --> 00:52:35.313
or Ko Ko Thett?

00:52:37.730 --> 00:52:39.300
- Yeah, first of all,

00:52:39.300 --> 00:52:41.390
I would like to appreciate Ma Thida,

00:52:41.390 --> 00:52:45.353
first, because of her work of
"Sanchaung, Insein, Harvard."

00:52:45.353 --> 00:52:49.990
Harvard is the first thing
that came to my mind,

00:52:49.990 --> 00:52:52.118
when I think of literature and resistance.

00:52:52.118 --> 00:52:55.640
Although she wrote about her experience

00:52:55.640 --> 00:52:57.620
as a political prisoner,

00:52:57.620 --> 00:52:59.803
after reading her book,

00:53:01.150 --> 00:53:04.360
rather than being afraid
of being sent to prison,

00:53:04.360 --> 00:53:08.756
I gained the bravery to go
challenged the presidency.

00:53:08.756 --> 00:53:12.506
So book can give us some courages,

00:53:12.506 --> 00:53:16.196
and regarding Ko Ko Thett,

00:53:16.196 --> 00:53:19.710
I'd like to honor his contribution

00:53:19.710 --> 00:53:21.740
that is to engage Burmese literature

00:53:21.740 --> 00:53:23.820
with the international communities,

00:53:23.820 --> 00:53:27.420
because the country was so isolated

00:53:27.420 --> 00:53:29.260
under the military regime,

00:53:29.260 --> 00:53:33.150
for almost two decades
after 1988 revolution.

00:53:33.150 --> 00:53:35.180
But now it's happening again.

00:53:35.180 --> 00:53:39.507
But you didn't leave us,
and arranged the book like,

00:53:39.507 --> 00:53:44.507
"Picking Off New Shoots
Will Not Stop the Spring."

00:53:44.629 --> 00:53:47.311
So thanks so much for that.

00:53:47.311 --> 00:53:51.061
So my question is so
simple, for Ko Ko Thett.

00:53:52.370 --> 00:53:56.391
So is there any challenge
you ever experienced,

00:53:56.391 --> 00:53:58.246
while you were arranging that book,

00:53:58.246 --> 00:54:02.329
I mean the "New Shoots
Will Not Stop the Spring?"

00:54:04.971 --> 00:54:09.347
And for Ma Thida, I know
now you are in jail,

00:54:09.347 --> 00:54:12.014
so may I know your recent works?

00:54:12.960 --> 00:54:17.960
Yeah, what are you doing there like that?

00:54:19.577 --> 00:54:21.910
- Yes, regarding curating of

00:54:24.281 --> 00:54:28.448
"Picking Off New Shoots
Will Not Stop the Spring."

00:54:29.447 --> 00:54:30.800
The first challenge was,

00:54:30.800 --> 00:54:35.800
I mean, how to collect material.

00:54:36.410 --> 00:54:38.310
And in the beginning, February, March,

00:54:42.270 --> 00:54:44.980
my co-editor Brian Haman and myself,

00:54:44.980 --> 00:54:47.530
we were very much assisted
by PEN Myanmar, Ma Thida

00:54:48.910 --> 00:54:52.433
and got lots of contributions
via PEN Myanmar.

00:54:53.590 --> 00:54:58.590
And later I had to scan the internet

00:54:58.724 --> 00:55:01.080
to get quality writing.

00:55:01.080 --> 00:55:03.530
And also another challenge is diversity.

00:55:03.530 --> 00:55:06.760
I would like to include
a polyphony of voices

00:55:06.760 --> 00:55:08.330
in the collection.

00:55:08.330 --> 00:55:10.770
So we had to reach out
to other resource persons

00:55:10.770 --> 00:55:15.020
who might know, this or that
ethnic writer and so on.

00:55:15.020 --> 00:55:18.130
So that way, we were able to include

00:55:18.130 --> 00:55:19.950
so many different voices in the book.

00:55:19.950 --> 00:55:22.486
Another challenge is to
get it done in one year.

00:55:22.486 --> 00:55:24.370
Because, most of the translation

00:55:24.370 --> 00:55:28.110
was done by me, single handedly,

00:55:28.110 --> 00:55:29.680
even though there are several other pieces

00:55:29.680 --> 00:55:30.870
written in English.

00:55:30.870 --> 00:55:33.420
So that was a full-time job,

00:55:33.420 --> 00:55:37.000
almost from March to September.

00:55:37.000 --> 00:55:38.200
So that was a challenge.

00:55:40.753 --> 00:55:42.797
- Yes, sayar ma Ma Thida

00:55:42.797 --> 00:55:43.980
- Thank you.

00:55:43.980 --> 00:55:47.910
Indeed, I was about to read a small poem,

00:55:47.910 --> 00:55:50.060
it's called, "Get Out of The Maze.

00:55:50.060 --> 00:55:51.825
That's what I'm doing here.

00:55:51.825 --> 00:55:54.920
I'm thinking of writing a new novel,

00:55:54.920 --> 00:55:57.040
but in order to write that novel

00:55:57.040 --> 00:55:59.590
I need a lot of information.

00:55:59.590 --> 00:56:03.730
So I have been closely
watching my own country,

00:56:03.730 --> 00:56:04.820
what's going on,

00:56:04.820 --> 00:56:07.030
and not just currently,

00:56:07.030 --> 00:56:10.060
but also during the last decades.

00:56:10.060 --> 00:56:12.283
I trying to understand,

00:56:13.270 --> 00:56:16.690
I also here at YALE,

00:56:16.690 --> 00:56:20.360
I try to reach out to
that diaspora population,

00:56:20.360 --> 00:56:24.900
either in person or through the internet.

00:56:24.900 --> 00:56:27.480
I reach out to them
and I try to understand

00:56:27.480 --> 00:56:29.440
the whole picture what's going on.

00:56:29.440 --> 00:56:34.440
And I'm going to start my new book,

00:56:34.470 --> 00:56:36.640
but still struggling about the structure,

00:56:36.640 --> 00:56:40.340
because my dream is quite a little big.

00:56:40.340 --> 00:56:44.600
And then the atrocity
doesn't stop, it's going on.

00:56:44.600 --> 00:56:49.600
So I might decide the
period to end for my piece,

00:56:50.670 --> 00:56:54.033
so that will be my future book.

00:56:56.467 --> 00:56:58.293
That will be amazed.

00:57:02.570 --> 00:57:04.250
- Thank you so much.

00:57:04.250 --> 00:57:06.590
I wanna open up to open Q&amp;A

00:57:06.590 --> 00:57:09.460
from some of our
attendees in the audience.

00:57:09.460 --> 00:57:11.563
So if you would like to ask a question,

00:57:12.690 --> 00:57:13.750
just raise your hand,

00:57:13.750 --> 00:57:18.750
using the hand raise function
of the Zoom interface.

00:57:18.990 --> 00:57:19.980
If you can't find that,

00:57:19.980 --> 00:57:21.940
just wave your hand and
I'll try and see you,

00:57:21.940 --> 00:57:24.440
but you'll have to show
your video to do that one.

00:57:28.500 --> 00:57:30.180
I see a question from Al Lim

00:57:31.930 --> 00:57:32.763
- Hi everyone.

00:57:32.763 --> 00:57:34.586
Thank you so much for the
wonderful presentation.

00:57:34.586 --> 00:57:36.600
And it's great to see you.

00:57:36.600 --> 00:57:38.980
I think Ko Ko Thett
visited a few years ago

00:57:38.980 --> 00:57:41.760
and we had to have lunch
at some point there

00:57:41.760 --> 00:57:43.080
it was wonderful.

00:57:43.080 --> 00:57:46.440
So it's thank you again for talking about

00:57:46.440 --> 00:57:47.910
the kind of state of Burmese poetry

00:57:47.910 --> 00:57:50.340
and how y'all are gonna
contributing so well,

00:57:50.340 --> 00:57:53.830
both kind of in, in Myanmar
and internationally.

00:57:53.830 --> 00:57:55.910
I think my question is about
the state of Burmese poetry

00:57:55.910 --> 00:57:57.083
for like young people.

00:57:58.340 --> 00:58:00.440
I'm wondering, like, is it
a big thing in universities?

00:58:00.440 --> 00:58:01.810
Like how do people get into it?

00:58:01.810 --> 00:58:04.601
Can you just talk a little bit more about

00:58:04.601 --> 00:58:08.650
the state of Burmese poetry in Myanmar,

00:58:08.650 --> 00:58:12.040
both in Yangon and in other cities,

00:58:12.040 --> 00:58:13.880
and just as a part of comparison,

00:58:13.880 --> 00:58:14.720
like in Singapore,

00:58:14.720 --> 00:58:17.560
there's a big Singapore
poetry writing month

00:58:17.560 --> 00:58:19.360
that Joshua Ip, and a few folks run.

00:58:19.360 --> 00:58:21.372
And it's being taught in schools,

00:58:21.372 --> 00:58:23.680
so that's one way people get into it.

00:58:23.680 --> 00:58:24.513
Whereas in Thailand,

00:58:24.513 --> 00:58:27.310
it's not as big for like
young people, I feel,

00:58:27.310 --> 00:58:29.100
where it's a lot more kind of like formal

00:58:29.100 --> 00:58:32.090
and very strict and rigid.

00:58:32.090 --> 00:58:33.296
And so I'm just wondering,

00:58:33.296 --> 00:58:35.250
how did you know people get into it?

00:58:35.250 --> 00:58:36.083
It's great to hear from you.

00:58:36.083 --> 00:58:39.900
I'm just curious about how
vibrant it is as a community.

00:58:39.900 --> 00:58:40.733
Thank you.

00:58:42.190 --> 00:58:47.190
- Yes, I can speak of poetry
as a curriculum in school,

00:58:47.490 --> 00:58:49.810
which is actually hopeless,

00:58:49.810 --> 00:58:51.710
because Burmese school curriculum,

00:58:51.710 --> 00:58:54.610
they hardly teach any free thinking.

00:58:57.750 --> 00:59:00.350
So most of the poems are quite rigid,

00:59:00.350 --> 00:59:04.070
and they don't teach
contemporary poetry at all.

00:59:04.070 --> 00:59:07.030
Most of the poets are
dead and in long gone.

00:59:07.030 --> 00:59:08.280
So that's one thing.

00:59:08.280 --> 00:59:10.480
Maybe that's the reason
why on the other hand,

00:59:10.480 --> 00:59:14.560
there's a vibrant,
unofficial learning of poetry

00:59:14.560 --> 00:59:17.710
and other modes of literature in Burma.

00:59:17.710 --> 00:59:22.410
So I often speak of this
participatory poetry

00:59:22.410 --> 00:59:25.320
written by ordinary people
who are not necessarily poets,

00:59:25.320 --> 00:59:27.267
because our Burmese language

00:59:27.267 --> 00:59:29.820
also is quite the rhyming language.

00:59:29.820 --> 00:59:34.820
And if you are observing
the protests even in 2021,

00:59:36.880 --> 00:59:41.400
most of the protest
slogans were in couplets

00:59:41.400 --> 00:59:44.020
written by participatory poets.

00:59:44.020 --> 00:59:49.020
Very powerful and very in
your face, against the regime.

00:59:49.280 --> 00:59:54.027
So these are very
impressive lines, such as

00:59:54.027 --> 00:59:55.540
(speaking in foreign language)

00:59:55.540 --> 00:59:57.583
No negotiation for blood debt.

00:59:58.560 --> 01:00:02.143
And even "Picking Off New Shoots
Will Not Stop the Spring,"

01:00:02.143 --> 01:00:07.143
that come from the protests of Burma.

01:00:07.370 --> 01:00:09.160
So anyway, that's one thing.

01:00:09.160 --> 01:00:10.160
And on the other hand,

01:00:10.160 --> 01:00:14.550
there are career poets
and professional poets.

01:00:14.550 --> 01:00:15.820
And Ma Thida said,

01:00:15.820 --> 01:00:18.275
in different cities and towns,

01:00:18.275 --> 01:00:19.475
there are centers for...

01:00:22.680 --> 01:00:23.590
How shall I say it?

01:00:23.590 --> 01:00:27.540
There are literary groups, poetry groups,

01:00:27.540 --> 01:00:30.720
who usually gather at
tea shops, coffee shops,

01:00:30.720 --> 01:00:35.720
and they just discuss their poems.

01:00:35.830 --> 01:00:39.240
And just like workshops in the West,

01:00:39.240 --> 01:00:41.630
but they do usually
workshop each other poems,

01:00:41.630 --> 01:00:46.054
but still that's in different cities,

01:00:46.054 --> 01:00:48.280
there're different centers of poetry.

01:00:48.280 --> 01:00:53.160
Like Monywa has a very
vibrant center of poetry.

01:00:53.160 --> 01:00:57.193
And we lost two Monywa
poets in 2021 uprising.

01:01:03.030 --> 01:01:04.180
- Thank you Ko Ko Thett.

01:01:04.180 --> 01:01:07.050
Paul Sarno, you can ask a question.

01:01:07.050 --> 01:01:08.943
- This is directed to Ma Thida.

01:01:10.308 --> 01:01:14.310
I read and found very informative

01:01:14.310 --> 01:01:17.220
your book called "Prisoner of Conscience."

01:01:17.220 --> 01:01:20.540
And I understand you've written
a second book in English.

01:01:20.540 --> 01:01:23.740
I can't seem to find it
on the world catalog.

01:01:23.740 --> 01:01:25.650
Can you tell me, please,

01:01:25.650 --> 01:01:29.850
the English title and the
publisher, if you know.

01:01:29.850 --> 01:01:32.470
- Yes, the book is called "The Roadmap,"

01:01:32.470 --> 01:01:34.090
but it might appear

01:01:34.090 --> 01:01:37.603
under the name of my
pseudonym, Suragamika,

01:01:38.490 --> 01:01:39.920
the same publisher,

01:01:39.920 --> 01:01:43.810
the Silkworm Publication
from Chiang Mai, Thailand.

01:01:43.810 --> 01:01:46.660
But that was the earlier a book.

01:01:46.660 --> 01:01:48.617
I wrote that book in English.

01:01:48.617 --> 01:01:50.900
"The Roadmap," I wrote
that book in English.

01:01:50.900 --> 01:01:53.800
At that time, because of the situation,

01:01:53.800 --> 01:01:55.970
I dare not to use my own name,

01:01:55.970 --> 01:01:58.860
so I used a pseudonym Suragamika,.

01:01:58.860 --> 01:02:02.580
And then that book was written at Brown,

01:02:02.580 --> 01:02:05.840
and I moved to Redcliff Harvard next year,

01:02:05.840 --> 01:02:09.630
and I finished writing my
prison memoir there, in Burmese,

01:02:09.630 --> 01:02:12.877
but that the translation was published,

01:02:12.877 --> 01:02:14.740
as "A Prisoner Conscience."

01:02:14.740 --> 01:02:16.220
- Thank you.

01:02:16.220 --> 01:02:18.903
- How do you spell Sira, S-I-R-

01:02:19.770 --> 01:02:24.770
- Sura, S-U-R-A-G-A-M-I-K-A, Suragamika.

01:02:26.600 --> 01:02:29.000
It's kind of a Pali words,

01:02:29.000 --> 01:02:32.711
Sura means brave, Gamika is a traveler.

01:02:32.711 --> 01:02:35.183
- Suragamika.
- Yes.

01:02:35.183 --> 01:02:36.283
- A with a K.
- A, K.

01:02:38.553 --> 01:02:39.474
- K.
- K.

01:02:39.474 --> 01:02:41.030
- Okay, thank you.

01:02:41.030 --> 01:02:44.320
Also, if I can ask one more question?

01:02:44.320 --> 01:02:49.320
I wondered about how many
poets, writers that you know of

01:02:51.890 --> 01:02:55.133
have left Myanmar, since the coup?

01:02:58.314 --> 01:03:02.760
More than 20, more than 30, how many?

01:03:02.760 --> 01:03:05.440
- It's hard to say most of them,

01:03:05.440 --> 01:03:09.477
for example, like five were killed,

01:03:11.624 --> 01:03:14.457
and round about 14 were in prison,

01:03:17.963 --> 01:03:20.900
and then a lot has been left,

01:03:20.900 --> 01:03:24.490
but most of the writers and poets

01:03:24.490 --> 01:03:28.493
are resisting from the land, from inside.

01:03:29.401 --> 01:03:31.280
They keep doing.

01:03:31.280 --> 01:03:33.413
- Have they joined the P the...

01:03:39.630 --> 01:03:40.463
Oh, I'm sorry,

01:03:40.463 --> 01:03:42.000
I can't remember the abbreviation,

01:03:42.000 --> 01:03:46.797
but the forces that are
the fighting the junta.

01:03:46.797 --> 01:03:48.397
- PDF?
- Yeah, I'm sorry.

01:03:48.397 --> 01:03:50.727
The PDF of the NG.

01:03:50.727 --> 01:03:52.650
Have they joined them?

01:03:53.740 --> 01:03:56.170
- Yes, some already joined.

01:03:56.170 --> 01:03:58.210
Yeah, some already joined the PDF.

01:03:58.210 --> 01:03:59.940
And, you know, the minister

01:03:59.940 --> 01:04:04.580
of the Ministry of Defense
of NUG, himself is a poet.

01:04:04.580 --> 01:04:06.620
- Ah, okay.

01:04:06.620 --> 01:04:07.799
Thank you so much.

01:04:07.799 --> 01:04:08.900
(speaking in foreign language)

01:04:08.900 --> 01:04:10.607
- Thank you.

01:04:18.890 --> 01:04:20.420
- Do we have any other questions?

01:04:20.420 --> 01:04:24.020
I have one, but I don't wanna
keep asking the only question.

01:04:24.020 --> 01:04:25.713
I wanna open it up to others.

01:04:28.500 --> 01:04:29.993
Eric White, we have question.

01:04:32.030 --> 01:04:33.240
- Hi, thanks.

01:04:33.240 --> 01:04:36.660
So, in general, there's
been a lot of discussion

01:04:36.660 --> 01:04:38.280
about since the coup,

01:04:38.280 --> 01:04:43.180
there's been greater engagement,
reproachment and solidarity

01:04:43.180 --> 01:04:47.260
between the ethnic Burman
majority populations

01:04:47.260 --> 01:04:49.520
and the ethnic minority populations.

01:04:49.520 --> 01:04:51.150
And I'm just wondering,

01:04:51.150 --> 01:04:55.240
do we see similar dynamics

01:04:55.240 --> 01:04:59.920
within the community of
writers of poetry and prose

01:04:59.920 --> 01:05:03.930
in the sense of as communities of writers,

01:05:03.930 --> 01:05:08.173
as communities of readers,
as community of commentators?

01:05:10.499 --> 01:05:12.987
So just wondering how
of the coup has changed

01:05:15.390 --> 01:05:16.340
the character actually,

01:05:16.340 --> 01:05:18.830
of communities of writers and readers

01:05:18.830 --> 01:05:23.830
inside of Myanmar and across
ethnic kinds of boundaries?

01:05:30.760 --> 01:05:32.700
- I think, I answer your question.

01:05:32.700 --> 01:05:33.583
It's hard.

01:05:33.583 --> 01:05:36.850
That's why PEN Myanmar has a plan

01:05:36.850 --> 01:05:41.590
to have a short story writing Workshop

01:05:46.625 --> 01:05:47.840
with minorities,

01:05:47.840 --> 01:05:52.840
especially we pick Kayah, Mon,

01:05:53.280 --> 01:05:57.373
Chin and Kachin and Shan.

01:05:58.220 --> 01:06:01.230
So that's the program.

01:06:01.230 --> 01:06:04.133
According to our survey in 2018,

01:06:07.500 --> 01:06:12.500
there is almost very few

01:06:12.600 --> 01:06:14.360
from the ethnic minority

01:06:14.360 --> 01:06:16.163
becoming a well known writer.

01:06:17.440 --> 01:06:22.180
Even though we do have a couple
of the active communication

01:06:24.350 --> 01:06:28.580
between these ethnic
minorities, it's very hard.

01:06:28.580 --> 01:06:32.690
That's why I think the ethnic literature

01:06:32.690 --> 01:06:35.620
is almost not existing.

01:06:35.620 --> 01:06:37.760
For example, like in Kachin language,

01:06:37.760 --> 01:06:40.850
there is only the translation of the Bible

01:06:40.850 --> 01:06:42.608
into Kachin language.

01:06:42.608 --> 01:06:45.070
There is not much other literature,

01:06:45.070 --> 01:06:50.070
even though they do have kind of video

01:06:50.240 --> 01:06:54.270
or the feature movie, something like that,

01:06:54.270 --> 01:06:56.098
but not in literature form.

01:06:56.098 --> 01:07:01.098
So it's very hard only
in Shan and Mon language

01:07:02.080 --> 01:07:04.460
have their own literature,

01:07:04.460 --> 01:07:06.330
but it's not very popular,

01:07:06.330 --> 01:07:08.510
it's never been translated into Burmese

01:07:08.510 --> 01:07:11.060
to reach out to the broader audience.

01:07:11.060 --> 01:07:14.070
So it's very hard to make a communication

01:07:14.070 --> 01:07:18.620
between the writers
written in Burmese language

01:07:18.620 --> 01:07:21.860
with the writers, written in
other different languages.

01:07:21.860 --> 01:07:24.930
Only we PEN Myanmar try to reach out,

01:07:24.930 --> 01:07:27.650
but it's not yet successful,

01:07:27.650 --> 01:07:28.483
because of the coup.

01:07:28.483 --> 01:07:33.320
We had planned to have a short
story training in Shan State

01:07:33.320 --> 01:07:35.100
on 22nd February,

01:07:35.100 --> 01:07:37.073
and it was banned.

01:07:39.180 --> 01:07:42.090
I think Jane wants to add something.

01:07:42.090 --> 01:07:45.343
She's a part in Shan, even
though I'm Shan, I don't.

01:07:47.250 --> 01:07:48.083
- Go ahead, Jane.

01:07:48.083 --> 01:07:48.940
And while you're talking,

01:07:48.940 --> 01:07:50.842
I'm going to announce
your talk next week too.

01:07:50.842 --> 01:07:53.613
I'll put it in the chat, so people.

01:07:54.810 --> 01:07:56.498
- Yeah, thank you for...

01:07:56.498 --> 01:07:58.743
Oh, am I frozen now?

01:08:01.600 --> 01:08:03.530
- Oh, just for a second.

01:08:03.530 --> 01:08:04.906
Unfrozen now.

01:08:04.906 --> 01:08:09.360
- Yeah, there is the thing, chilled.

01:08:09.360 --> 01:08:12.700
So the longest continuously operating

01:08:12.700 --> 01:08:15.490
Shan printing press in Taunggyi,

01:08:15.490 --> 01:08:18.901
has a huge Pantheon of Shan poetry,

01:08:18.901 --> 01:08:22.343
Shan short stories, comic books,

01:08:23.330 --> 01:08:25.327
that have been continuously operating,

01:08:25.327 --> 01:08:28.730
but then also in the Shan
liberated territories

01:08:28.730 --> 01:08:31.960
from the seventies, eighties,
and nineties onwards,

01:08:31.960 --> 01:08:36.670
they operated a lead Linotype
type setting machines.

01:08:36.670 --> 01:08:38.740
So the constant production,

01:08:38.740 --> 01:08:40.310
and with the digital age,

01:08:40.310 --> 01:08:42.362
there's a whole lot out there.

01:08:42.362 --> 01:08:46.760
But also specifically
because this revolutionary,

01:08:46.760 --> 01:08:48.180
the ongoing civil war

01:08:48.180 --> 01:08:51.270
and the ongoing resistance in Shan State

01:08:51.270 --> 01:08:55.750
is in some ways separate
from the activities

01:08:55.750 --> 01:09:00.380
that have been going on against the coup

01:09:00.380 --> 01:09:01.730
in the Burmese Heartland.

01:09:01.730 --> 01:09:06.050
So there's a political
different orientation,

01:09:06.050 --> 01:09:10.160
just to summarize it very, very simply.

01:09:10.160 --> 01:09:14.610
But especially about Shan
revolutionary singers,

01:09:14.610 --> 01:09:19.010
some of them will make
Burmese versions of the songs.

01:09:19.010 --> 01:09:21.340
So if we want to broaden poetry

01:09:21.340 --> 01:09:25.040
to include rock and roll and songs,

01:09:25.040 --> 01:09:27.930
there's a tremendous amount of crossover

01:09:27.930 --> 01:09:29.070
between Shan artists

01:09:29.070 --> 01:09:32.970
and making songs that are accessible

01:09:32.970 --> 01:09:36.380
to Burma people who don't know Shan.

01:09:36.380 --> 01:09:40.003
So the language and
the political division,

01:09:40.003 --> 01:09:43.490
can be transgressed in really fascinating

01:09:43.490 --> 01:09:44.873
and revolutionary ways.

01:09:47.940 --> 01:09:49.047
- Go ahead Thawda.

01:09:53.270 --> 01:09:55.020
- Yeah, I would like to add something.

01:09:55.020 --> 01:09:59.810
Even though we are not engaging

01:09:59.810 --> 01:10:02.880
our ethnic or communities in the coup.

01:10:02.880 --> 01:10:06.670
Before the coup, as much as I remembered,

01:10:06.670 --> 01:10:10.660
we had a poetry event two years ago.

01:10:10.660 --> 01:10:15.660
In that poetry event, we
brought the Rohingya poets,

01:10:16.630 --> 01:10:17.893
to recite the poems.

01:10:21.953 --> 01:10:24.000
To reveal the experience.

01:10:24.000 --> 01:10:27.177
And then even in the new book of

01:10:27.177 --> 01:10:29.250
"Picking Off New Shoots,"

01:10:29.250 --> 01:10:31.630
I think some of the writers,

01:10:31.630 --> 01:10:34.583
even though they are
not the ethnic people,

01:10:36.057 --> 01:10:40.040
I mean they're just Burma.

01:10:40.040 --> 01:10:42.730
I think some of their writers

01:10:42.730 --> 01:10:45.380
can cover other ethnic experience.

01:10:45.380 --> 01:10:48.100
Like the poem of Cathy.

01:10:48.100 --> 01:10:52.050
I think Ko Ko Thett can
also share that poem,

01:10:52.050 --> 01:10:54.880
(speaking foreign language)

01:10:54.880 --> 01:10:56.890
Was written by Cathy.

01:10:56.890 --> 01:10:59.170
His poem is really amazing,

01:10:59.170 --> 01:11:01.750
and I think there is really engaging

01:11:01.750 --> 01:11:04.843
with the other minorities
experience and sufferings.

01:11:08.818 --> 01:11:09.651
(speaking foreign language)

01:11:09.651 --> 01:11:14.651
- By Cathy is not in the
collection anthology,

01:11:15.175 --> 01:11:16.950
"Picking of New Shoots
Will Not Stop the Spring,"

01:11:16.950 --> 01:11:19.593
but a translation of
that poem is published

01:11:19.593 --> 01:11:20.600
in the research,

01:11:20.600 --> 01:11:25.600
the channel of Burma research
that's out, I think last week.

01:11:27.543 --> 01:11:30.142
So there's that poem there,

01:11:30.142 --> 01:11:32.830
but in "Picking Off New Shoots
Will Not Stop the Spring,"

01:11:32.830 --> 01:11:34.229
anthology itself,

01:11:34.229 --> 01:11:38.650
there are three Rohingya
poets from different places,

01:11:38.650 --> 01:11:42.400
Cox's Bazaar, Malaysia
refugee camp and Canada.

01:11:42.400 --> 01:11:45.300
So we are quite happy and honored

01:11:45.300 --> 01:11:50.300
that they are contributing
those poems to this anthology.

01:11:57.570 --> 01:12:01.250
- So I wanted to ask a
question for all three of you

01:12:02.290 --> 01:12:06.920
about the relationship
between content and form

01:12:06.920 --> 01:12:10.710
in periods of resistance.

01:12:10.710 --> 01:12:14.690
And the question that has
long been on my mind...

01:12:14.690 --> 01:12:17.430
Actually before the events in Myanmar

01:12:18.360 --> 01:12:21.110
with my work in Vietnam,

01:12:21.110 --> 01:12:23.490
one of the burdens that some of my friends

01:12:23.490 --> 01:12:24.980
who are Vietnamese artists

01:12:24.980 --> 01:12:26.990
often described to me,

01:12:26.990 --> 01:12:31.530
is how as artists, they
want to be experimental

01:12:31.530 --> 01:12:34.200
in particularly artistic ways

01:12:34.200 --> 01:12:36.900
that are not always
driven by telling a story

01:12:36.900 --> 01:12:39.596
about Vietnam for example.

01:12:39.596 --> 01:12:44.596
Their artistic expression
transcends Vietnam nest,

01:12:44.830 --> 01:12:45.663
or something like,

01:12:45.663 --> 01:12:46.700
or Vietnamese nest,

01:12:46.700 --> 01:12:50.340
or telling the story of "the Vietnam war"

01:12:50.340 --> 01:12:51.840
or something of that.

01:12:51.840 --> 01:12:53.363
And I've always been curious,

01:12:54.340 --> 01:12:56.450
even with the title of this panel

01:12:56.450 --> 01:12:58.324
of Resistance and Literature,

01:12:58.324 --> 01:13:01.010
what it's like to be a writer

01:13:01.010 --> 01:13:05.400
who wants to play with language
for the sake of language?

01:13:05.400 --> 01:13:08.090
Ko Ko Thett, you mentioned
some of the couplet forms

01:13:08.090 --> 01:13:12.250
or some of the literary
styles of Burmese language.

01:13:12.250 --> 01:13:13.500
Ma Thida mentioned,

01:13:13.500 --> 01:13:14.640
when she was younger,

01:13:14.640 --> 01:13:17.190
when she first got excited
about being a writer

01:13:17.190 --> 01:13:20.350
as just the beauty of the language itself.

01:13:20.350 --> 01:13:22.670
Sometimes that's political,

01:13:22.670 --> 01:13:23.820
and sometimes it's not.

01:13:23.820 --> 01:13:26.763
Sometimes it's just artistic expression.

01:13:27.810 --> 01:13:29.530
But Ko Ko Thett you also mentioned

01:13:29.530 --> 01:13:32.680
how they're the rigid form
of certain forms of poetry,

01:13:32.680 --> 01:13:34.130
has also linked to a kind of

01:13:34.130 --> 01:13:37.010
repressive educational system too.

01:13:37.010 --> 01:13:39.610
And so I wondered if the
three of you could reflect

01:13:39.610 --> 01:13:44.200
just a little bit about
artistic expression

01:13:44.200 --> 01:13:47.410
and the burden of representing resistance,

01:13:47.410 --> 01:13:49.277
and how you manage that a little bit

01:13:49.277 --> 01:13:50.853
and how you engage with that.

01:13:56.520 --> 01:13:59.730
- Yes, I meant to say
that the rigid form itself

01:13:59.730 --> 01:14:00.620
is not to blame.

01:14:00.620 --> 01:14:03.410
I mean, when you write in the rigid form,

01:14:03.410 --> 01:14:06.413
it can lead to uncertain turn.

01:14:07.260 --> 01:14:11.750
But, what I meant is that
the curriculum itself

01:14:12.807 --> 01:14:16.180
that makes the rigid form.

01:14:16.180 --> 01:14:18.800
And then of course the curriculum itself

01:14:19.920 --> 01:14:24.620
is full of poems, that nationalist
kind of poems and so on,

01:14:24.620 --> 01:14:29.620
this makes it very problematic
to become a creative writer.

01:14:30.910 --> 01:14:35.190
But when it comes to
writing and resistance,

01:14:35.190 --> 01:14:40.190
I like to quote the Peruvian
novelist, Mario Vargas Llosa.

01:14:41.500 --> 01:14:45.237
He said that, "The mere
existence of literature

01:14:45.237 --> 01:14:47.517
"is a subversive act."

01:14:48.800 --> 01:14:52.290
For instance, Burmese novelist Sei Sei

01:14:52.290 --> 01:14:53.580
in the 1950s,

01:14:53.580 --> 01:14:56.463
wrote her memoirs about
the stigma of syphilis.

01:14:58.430 --> 01:14:59.410
That was in the 1950s.

01:14:59.410 --> 01:15:01.820
And the book in my opinion,

01:15:01.820 --> 01:15:04.110
was a subversive act,

01:15:04.110 --> 01:15:07.430
in a time syphilis was
looked down as a curse

01:15:07.430 --> 01:15:08.543
or one's bad karma.

01:15:10.098 --> 01:15:13.470
So we can actually write
about personal experience

01:15:13.470 --> 01:15:14.713
as personal stigma,

01:15:15.646 --> 01:15:20.220
and becomes subversive in our
own culture in our own way.

01:15:20.220 --> 01:15:25.220
So for me, the only responsibility
for writers is to write,

01:15:25.750 --> 01:15:27.810
and beyond that they
should not feel any burden

01:15:27.810 --> 01:15:30.310
or any expeditions from anyone,

01:15:30.310 --> 01:15:33.280
because I think resistance writing,

01:15:33.280 --> 01:15:35.260
just like all other creative activities

01:15:35.260 --> 01:15:39.960
must come from within
oneself, not from the outside.

01:15:39.960 --> 01:15:41.280
So a piece of writing...

01:15:41.280 --> 01:15:43.890
In my opinion, a piece of writing

01:15:43.890 --> 01:15:45.150
that responds to a groups

01:15:45.150 --> 01:15:48.170
or an individual's political expectations

01:15:48.170 --> 01:15:50.873
is rhetoric or propaganda.

01:15:53.854 --> 01:15:56.070
- I think, yeah, I also.

01:15:59.340 --> 01:16:03.580
I published a book it's
called "My Writing."

01:16:03.580 --> 01:16:07.420
Because it has been
pretty much contradictory

01:16:08.398 --> 01:16:12.657
to have so many people saying,

01:16:12.657 --> 01:16:16.127
"Oh, your piece of writing is a poetry,

01:16:16.127 --> 01:16:18.727
"or an essay, or a short story.

01:16:18.727 --> 01:16:20.230
"We don't know what it is."

01:16:20.230 --> 01:16:22.927
And I said, "Okay, I also
don't know what it is,

01:16:22.927 --> 01:16:24.654
"because I'm not an academy.

01:16:24.654 --> 01:16:27.330
"I just create it.

01:16:27.330 --> 01:16:31.079
So let's call my writing, that's all.

01:16:31.079 --> 01:16:32.530
(speaking foreign language)

01:16:32.530 --> 01:16:33.620
And that one,

01:16:33.620 --> 01:16:35.680
it's pretty much like

01:16:35.680 --> 01:16:39.503
focusing on the strength
of the language itself.

01:16:41.530 --> 01:16:45.590
It might reflect some political issue,

01:16:45.590 --> 01:16:47.803
because I think as a writer,

01:16:49.410 --> 01:16:53.710
we just use our own
individual way of response

01:16:53.710 --> 01:16:55.410
to the universe.

01:16:55.410 --> 01:17:00.410
So if the universe surrounding
us is pretty much political,

01:17:00.980 --> 01:17:03.860
our response will be
more or less political.

01:17:03.860 --> 01:17:06.180
There is no way to escape from it.

01:17:06.180 --> 01:17:07.370
That's all.

01:17:07.370 --> 01:17:12.370
We just reflecting whatever
we are surrounded by,

01:17:13.860 --> 01:17:14.700
something like that.

01:17:14.700 --> 01:17:18.343
So even though as an individual writer,

01:17:20.680 --> 01:17:21.930
of course our strength,

01:17:21.930 --> 01:17:26.930
it's also not just our perspective
towards something else,

01:17:29.820 --> 01:17:33.503
but also the skills,

01:17:34.840 --> 01:17:39.840
the aesthetic, intrinsic
part of the aestheticness,

01:17:40.370 --> 01:17:41.790
it's also our strength.

01:17:41.790 --> 01:17:45.590
So that's directly really to how much

01:17:45.590 --> 01:17:48.810
we can play with the language,

01:17:48.810 --> 01:17:51.840
the linguist parts of our own languages.

01:17:51.840 --> 01:17:55.110
That's why I think this is very much...

01:17:55.110 --> 01:17:57.640
When we talk about the freedom expression,

01:17:57.640 --> 01:18:00.240
we need to talk about the language rights.

01:18:00.240 --> 01:18:03.540
So that's how I see,

01:18:03.540 --> 01:18:06.403
and I enjoy as a writer.

01:18:08.145 --> 01:18:11.613
We are the owner of our own
reflection towards the universe,

01:18:16.200 --> 01:18:18.820
- Yes, for me, I would like to mention

01:18:18.820 --> 01:18:21.810
about social resistant through literature,

01:18:21.810 --> 01:18:25.600
rather than the resistant
to the dictatorship.

01:18:25.600 --> 01:18:30.170
Because I think I could say
that bringing social justice

01:18:30.170 --> 01:18:32.810
and eliminating social inequalities,

01:18:32.810 --> 01:18:37.220
is much more difficult than
it is to end the dictatorship,

01:18:37.220 --> 01:18:41.570
because that discriminatory
norms and values

01:18:41.570 --> 01:18:46.570
are deeply rooted in our
society for many centuries.

01:18:46.830 --> 01:18:51.230
That's why, when I'm
trying to write something

01:18:52.160 --> 01:18:54.503
that is to deconstruct the norms,

01:18:55.380 --> 01:18:58.680
I have to cautious and not to use

01:18:58.680 --> 01:19:01.930
the old words repetitively,

01:19:01.930 --> 01:19:05.100
because of by using this again, again,

01:19:05.100 --> 01:19:09.110
I'm afraid of strengthening
or reinforcing that words.

01:19:09.110 --> 01:19:12.340
That's why I try to
interchange with other words.

01:19:12.340 --> 01:19:17.340
For example, in my second
novel "A West Minded Tomorrow,"

01:19:17.350 --> 01:19:21.470
it is about a young woman who
caused psychological trauma

01:19:21.470 --> 01:19:22.810
since she was young,

01:19:22.810 --> 01:19:26.650
because of her sexual
violence of her cousin,

01:19:26.650 --> 01:19:30.150
as well as abandoned of her father.

01:19:30.150 --> 01:19:32.026
So in that case,

01:19:32.026 --> 01:19:33.970
the people surrounding her said,

01:19:35.380 --> 01:19:38.070
that she suffering from hysteria,

01:19:38.070 --> 01:19:40.530
but she hates that word.

01:19:40.530 --> 01:19:42.958
What I want to say in that novel

01:19:42.958 --> 01:19:47.160
is the word hysteria is too gendered.

01:19:47.160 --> 01:19:48.410
In this framing,

01:19:48.410 --> 01:19:52.760
most people think it is
concerned only with women.

01:19:52.760 --> 01:19:55.890
And I also want to
explain how Myanmar women

01:19:55.890 --> 01:19:58.693
are afraid of being hysterical.

01:19:59.630 --> 01:20:00.743
So that's why,

01:20:01.882 --> 01:20:04.960
women, they keep their love expressions

01:20:04.960 --> 01:20:08.400
and never declared their
love before men do.

01:20:08.400 --> 01:20:12.920
And the story also tells
about how much difficult

01:20:12.920 --> 01:20:15.800
of Myanmar women to divorce their husband.

01:20:15.800 --> 01:20:19.406
So in that story, as I
wanted to deconstruct

01:20:19.406 --> 01:20:21.330
the term hysteria,

01:20:21.330 --> 01:20:23.800
I didn't use the word in the whole story.

01:20:23.800 --> 01:20:27.700
Instead, I tried to
either change other words,

01:20:27.700 --> 01:20:31.370
to just say that female
character suffering

01:20:31.370 --> 01:20:35.110
is just a kind of psychological
problems like others.

01:20:35.110 --> 01:20:38.100
It's not concerned with
female body and womb

01:20:38.100 --> 01:20:39.853
as we used to believe in the past.

01:20:41.069 --> 01:20:42.510
So one main problem is,

01:20:42.510 --> 01:20:45.378
whenever I get the all
norms to deconstruct,

01:20:45.378 --> 01:20:49.000
I have to be cautious of using the words,

01:20:49.000 --> 01:20:50.913
If I said to you before.

01:20:51.810 --> 01:20:53.649
So if we use the old terms,

01:20:53.649 --> 01:20:58.649
I'm afraid that meaning
relating to those specific words

01:20:58.760 --> 01:21:02.710
became repeated and it's like
reinforcing the meanings.

01:21:02.710 --> 01:21:04.910
That's why I have to
be cautious about that.

